WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.140
&gt;&gt; This session is about
continuous deployment.

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:10.650
What we're going to do is look at

00:00:10.651 --> 00:00:12.360
it from three perspectives.

00:00:12.361 --> 00:00:13.660
The sort of 10,000

00:00:13.661 --> 00:00:16.970
feet where we go Professor
Laurie Williams,

00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:18.990
and he's been working
with multiple

00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:20.550
companies as he's been

00:00:20.551 --> 00:00:22.350
running it so much that
we should talk about.

00:00:22.351 --> 00:00:24.210
So it's actually across

00:00:24.211 --> 00:00:26.460
industry on how they

00:00:26.461 --> 00:00:29.020
apply these methods on
the problems they have.

00:00:29.021 --> 00:00:34.065
We're also going to have a talk
by Bing, by shopaholics.

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:38.580
Bing is the classic continuous
deployment type product

00:00:38.581 --> 00:00:41.905
where you got service
with very little on-prem,

00:00:41.906 --> 00:00:45.185
so that's like the
1,000-foot perspective.

00:00:45.186 --> 00:00:47.945
Then finally, we
have Office which is

00:00:47.946 --> 00:00:51.305
probably the most difficult
thing to deploy,

00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:53.925
and Jen [inaudible] is going
to give a talk on that.

00:00:53.926 --> 00:00:57.240
Since it's sort of three talks
on very similar subject,

00:00:57.241 --> 00:00:58.940
then instead of taking

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:01.030
questions from each
of the speakers,

00:01:01.031 --> 00:01:03.260
what we're gonna do is we'll

00:01:03.261 --> 00:01:05.790
take no questions during
the each speakers,

00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:07.250
and then right at the end,

00:01:07.251 --> 00:01:08.915
we'll have all three speakers up

00:01:08.916 --> 00:01:10.600
and then we can ask questions
from the same thing.

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:11.620
Because you probably
will be asking

00:01:11.621 --> 00:01:13.540
roughly the same question from
each of the same speaker,

00:01:13.541 --> 00:01:14.900
so it's a lot easier
to just have them

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:16.625
all up and speaking.

00:01:16.626 --> 00:01:18.290
So first off, we have

00:01:18.291 --> 00:01:20.130
Professor Williams to talk

00:01:20.131 --> 00:01:22.395
about Challenges in
Continuous Deployment.

00:01:22.396 --> 00:01:27.080
&gt;&gt; Thanks Brandon. Thanks all
for coming to this session.

00:01:27.081 --> 00:01:29.860
Since you came to a continuous
deployment session,

00:01:29.861 --> 00:01:31.495
you probably know what it is.

00:01:31.496 --> 00:01:32.810
But I'm going to
start out just by

00:01:32.811 --> 00:01:34.670
defining it, just in case.

00:01:34.671 --> 00:01:37.010
So this is a pipeline,

00:01:37.011 --> 00:01:39.355
and with continuous deployment,

00:01:39.356 --> 00:01:41.835
when a developer makes a change,

00:01:41.836 --> 00:01:44.240
it goes through a number
of automated checks

00:01:44.241 --> 00:01:46.705
starting with unit tests,
could be code review,

00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:49.145
that could be a number of
different checks that are

00:01:49.146 --> 00:01:52.845
supposed to be happening
without any human intervention.

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:55.790
So it goes from a developer to

00:01:55.791 --> 00:01:58.430
actual customers, no
human intervention.

00:01:58.431 --> 00:02:01.720
It happens in some companies
in a matter of minutes.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.080
Like some companies deploy

00:02:04.081 --> 00:02:06.980
hundreds or even thousands
of times of day.

00:02:06.981 --> 00:02:09.030
Some it's less often.

00:02:09.031 --> 00:02:10.790
The point is to get
feedback back to

00:02:10.791 --> 00:02:12.940
the developers as
quickly as possible.

00:02:12.941 --> 00:02:15.245
But from a definition standpoint,

00:02:15.246 --> 00:02:18.755
continuous deployment says
no human intervention.

00:02:18.756 --> 00:02:22.460
Related continuous delivery is

00:02:22.461 --> 00:02:24.020
similar except there are

00:02:24.021 --> 00:02:26.075
some manual checks along the way.

00:02:26.076 --> 00:02:30.790
So I am randomly put
some manual steps in here,

00:02:30.791 --> 00:02:32.040
could be at any point,

00:02:32.041 --> 00:02:34.250
but continuous delivery says you

00:02:34.251 --> 00:02:37.100
haven't yet achieved
continuous deployment.

00:02:37.101 --> 00:02:39.020
From another term standpoint,

00:02:39.021 --> 00:02:41.520
you may hear also DevOps a lot.

00:02:41.521 --> 00:02:43.880
DevOps is an
organizational structure

00:02:43.881 --> 00:02:45.470
where the operations people who

00:02:45.471 --> 00:02:47.240
do the deployment collaborate

00:02:47.241 --> 00:02:49.620
much more closely
with the developers.

00:02:49.621 --> 00:02:52.280
So I'd say DevOps

00:02:52.281 --> 00:02:53.990
is an organizational
structure that would go

00:02:53.991 --> 00:02:55.745
around or could go around

00:02:55.746 --> 00:02:59.400
both continuous deployment
and continuous delivery.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.580
Brandon mentioned that I've
been running what I call

00:03:03.581 --> 00:03:06.045
the Continuous Deployment Summit

00:03:06.046 --> 00:03:09.320
in Silicon Valley over
the last four years.

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:10.990
On this slide are

00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:14.520
17 companies that have
participated in the summit.

00:03:14.521 --> 00:03:15.620
In the four years,

00:03:15.621 --> 00:03:16.880
they could've participated one,

00:03:16.881 --> 00:03:19.145
two, three or four times.

00:03:19.146 --> 00:03:21.330
If you look at the companies,

00:03:21.331 --> 00:03:23.175
a lot of them you'll know,

00:03:23.176 --> 00:03:24.420
some of them you won't,

00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:27.760
but we intentionally choose
companies for the summit

00:03:27.761 --> 00:03:29.530
that cross the spectrum of

00:03:29.531 --> 00:03:31.975
large company to small company,

00:03:31.976 --> 00:03:35.365
from companies that
I would say we're

00:03:35.366 --> 00:03:37.150
born doing continuous
delivery and

00:03:37.151 --> 00:03:39.270
continuous deployment
like Facebook,

00:03:39.271 --> 00:03:42.710
Netflix, Google, they never
did anything but that.

00:03:42.711 --> 00:03:44.910
Although they still
have challenges.

00:03:44.911 --> 00:03:48.520
To companies that like,
as Brandon mentioned,

00:03:48.521 --> 00:03:51.010
there's Windows, it's
a large monolithic product

00:03:51.011 --> 00:03:52.820
and Bing in the same company.

00:03:52.821 --> 00:03:54.510
Again, a spectrum.

00:03:54.511 --> 00:03:56.965
We have companies like Cisco,

00:03:56.966 --> 00:04:01.310
IBM, and SAS that have
large monolithic products.

00:04:01.311 --> 00:04:02.330
The uniting factor of

00:04:02.331 --> 00:04:04.985
all the companies is that
they all want to use

00:04:04.986 --> 00:04:07.010
continuous deployment types of

00:04:07.011 --> 00:04:09.870
practices to improve
their efficiency,

00:04:09.871 --> 00:04:12.475
to get value to the
customers quicker,

00:04:12.476 --> 00:04:15.115
and to get feedback
back to the developers.

00:04:15.116 --> 00:04:16.210
They all have struggles,

00:04:16.211 --> 00:04:18.740
even though the ones like
I said, Facebook, Google,

00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:20.440
Netflix that started that way,

00:04:20.441 --> 00:04:21.570
they still have struggles, they

00:04:21.571 --> 00:04:23.655
still want to be more efficient.

00:04:23.656 --> 00:04:25.920
As far as the years,

00:04:25.921 --> 00:04:28.370
the first year was in
Facebook, second year Google,

00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:30.770
third year Netflix, and Brandon

00:04:30.771 --> 00:04:34.010
hosted at Microsoft
just this past Monday.

00:04:34.011 --> 00:04:35.420
So I couldn't do my slides ahead

00:04:35.421 --> 00:04:37.225
of time because it just happened.

00:04:37.226 --> 00:04:40.610
So what I'll be sharing with
you are the challenges that

00:04:40.611 --> 00:04:42.025
the company has talked about

00:04:42.026 --> 00:04:44.470
in the Continuous
Deployment Summit.

00:04:44.471 --> 00:04:47.895
It's not just about
challenges though,

00:04:47.896 --> 00:04:49.850
there actually are some benefits.

00:04:49.851 --> 00:04:51.350
So I'll start out with
the benefits and then

00:04:51.351 --> 00:04:53.224
spend most of the time
on the challenges.

00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:55.010
So the companies that they

00:04:55.011 --> 00:04:57.050
use these practices
some of the benefits,

00:04:57.051 --> 00:04:59.000
first of all, relate
to the engineers.

00:04:59.001 --> 00:05:00.830
So the engineers are happier,

00:05:00.831 --> 00:05:02.365
they get feedback quicker,

00:05:02.366 --> 00:05:04.670
they feel more empowered,

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:07.420
and generally they
like working that way.

00:05:07.421 --> 00:05:10.330
Although we'll talk about
culture in a little bit.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.510
The company gets a lot
of value because they're

00:05:14.511 --> 00:05:17.870
continuously sending value
out to the customers,

00:05:17.871 --> 00:05:20.825
giving them new functionality
on a regular cadence.

00:05:20.826 --> 00:05:24.260
They don't have
big release stress

00:05:24.261 --> 00:05:26.365
because the releases are
happening all the time.

00:05:26.366 --> 00:05:28.780
If a function doesn't
make this release,

00:05:28.781 --> 00:05:30.340
it can make the one
this afternoon,

00:05:30.341 --> 00:05:33.135
and so you don't have
a type of release stress.

00:05:33.136 --> 00:05:36.545
The companies themselves,
the attendees,

00:05:36.546 --> 00:05:38.900
said that they feel they have

00:05:38.901 --> 00:05:41.865
quality and
productivity benefits,

00:05:41.866 --> 00:05:43.990
although we don't have
empirical evidence of that.

00:05:43.991 --> 00:05:45.540
But that's the way they felt.

00:05:45.541 --> 00:05:48.320
That the focus on
automation towards

00:05:48.321 --> 00:05:51.165
getting to the
continuous deployment,

00:05:51.166 --> 00:05:54.880
that automation- they
view it as a benefit,

00:05:54.881 --> 00:05:58.420
the focus on automating more
and more of the practices.

00:05:58.421 --> 00:06:01.640
Quicker mean time to repair,

00:06:01.641 --> 00:06:03.780
so if there is a problem,

00:06:03.781 --> 00:06:06.075
the ability to get a fix to

00:06:06.076 --> 00:06:08.490
a customer quick is a benefit.

00:06:08.491 --> 00:06:10.610
So they have this
deployment pipeline

00:06:10.611 --> 00:06:12.695
where they're continuously
getting change out,

00:06:12.696 --> 00:06:14.020
and so if there's a problem,

00:06:14.021 --> 00:06:15.260
they just follow the normal

00:06:15.261 --> 00:06:17.140
process and get the change out.

00:06:17.141 --> 00:06:18.980
To me, one of

00:06:18.981 --> 00:06:21.770
the biggest differences that

00:06:21.771 --> 00:06:24.560
you can see with that
is back it was 2014,

00:06:24.561 --> 00:06:25.985
so it was a while ago,

00:06:25.986 --> 00:06:29.280
there were security
problems found both with

00:06:29.281 --> 00:06:32.665
Jeeps and with Tesla,

00:06:32.666 --> 00:06:34.210
within two weeks of each other.

00:06:34.211 --> 00:06:35.780
So for the Jeep,

00:06:35.781 --> 00:06:37.595
when there was
a security problem,

00:06:37.596 --> 00:06:41.515
Chrysler sent USB sticks
out to all the customers,

00:06:41.516 --> 00:06:43.200
all the car owners, 1.4

00:06:43.201 --> 00:06:46.185
million USB sticks
went to car owners.

00:06:46.186 --> 00:06:49.395
What do you think happened
to those USB sticks?

00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:51.680
Right? How many of them
actually were used?

00:06:51.681 --> 00:06:52.810
How many of them
were thrown away?

00:06:52.811 --> 00:06:55.145
How many of them were repurposed?

00:06:55.146 --> 00:06:58.330
Within that same timeframe,
couple weeks later,

00:06:58.331 --> 00:07:00.480
Tesla had a different problem,

00:07:00.481 --> 00:07:02.005
but a security problem,

00:07:02.006 --> 00:07:03.445
they fixed it up over the air.

00:07:03.446 --> 00:07:06.700
So they have the ability
to do something like that,

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:08.530
and any of the
companies that have

00:07:08.531 --> 00:07:10.360
this continuous deployment
pipeline have

00:07:10.361 --> 00:07:12.955
the ability to get
fixes out quickly.

00:07:12.956 --> 00:07:16.250
Then, in general,
developers get feedback.

00:07:16.251 --> 00:07:17.420
So those are the benefits.

00:07:17.421 --> 00:07:20.500
Now we'll talk about
the challenges for companies.

00:07:20.501 --> 00:07:23.050
On the first challenge is,

00:07:23.051 --> 00:07:26.155
what happens when you
have a large change?

00:07:26.156 --> 00:07:28.454
So part of continuous deployment

00:07:28.455 --> 00:07:30.905
includes continuous integration.

00:07:30.906 --> 00:07:34.070
So when a developer
makes a change,

00:07:34.071 --> 00:07:36.140
they are always integrating.

00:07:36.141 --> 00:07:37.300
They're not keeping things on

00:07:37.301 --> 00:07:39.109
their own machine,
on the sandbox,

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.360
on a different branch,
they're actually

00:07:41.361 --> 00:07:45.580
integrating the change
into the main branch.

00:07:45.581 --> 00:07:48.540
So what happens when
you have a big change?

00:07:48.541 --> 00:07:50.925
Well, generally,
what is done is it's

00:07:50.926 --> 00:07:53.800
a feature toggle is put
around that change,

00:07:53.801 --> 00:07:57.410
so that the developer can
continue to integrate

00:07:57.411 --> 00:08:01.375
their changes without the
customer experience changing,

00:08:01.376 --> 00:08:05.660
and they can keep
developing that way.

00:08:05.661 --> 00:08:07.840
So I have architecture changes,

00:08:07.841 --> 00:08:09.230
any large feature changes,

00:08:09.231 --> 00:08:11.670
database changes are
all done this way.

00:08:11.671 --> 00:08:13.940
What this picture is
really showing is,

00:08:13.941 --> 00:08:15.650
and this is a little
bit of classic

00:08:15.651 --> 00:08:17.360
with a database schema change,

00:08:17.361 --> 00:08:18.740
that what can be
happening is you're

00:08:18.741 --> 00:08:20.720
using one database and

00:08:20.721 --> 00:08:24.200
then when you make a change
that has a schema change,

00:08:24.201 --> 00:08:26.640
then you put a feature
flag around it and,

00:08:26.641 --> 00:08:28.400
potentially, what's happening is

00:08:28.401 --> 00:08:30.470
now that for testing purposes,

00:08:30.471 --> 00:08:33.660
you're sending it to bolt
the paths at the same time,

00:08:33.661 --> 00:08:36.635
that bolt databases
are being populated.

00:08:36.636 --> 00:08:38.145
Then when you feel good about it,

00:08:38.146 --> 00:08:39.785
then you take
the old database away.

00:08:39.786 --> 00:08:41.370
That's similar to any kind

00:08:41.371 --> 00:08:44.445
of large architectural
change as well.

00:08:44.446 --> 00:08:46.069
You can be having

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.730
potentially traffic going
to the new architecture,

00:08:48.731 --> 00:08:50.400
you're testing
the new architecture,

00:08:50.401 --> 00:08:52.600
but the users can't see it yet.

00:08:52.601 --> 00:08:55.490
So it is a testing strategy
where you're testing in

00:08:55.491 --> 00:08:58.420
production which we'll
talk about more as well.

00:08:58.421 --> 00:09:00.815
But this is really
enabled by the use of

00:09:00.816 --> 00:09:04.845
feature toggles and the way
that you direct the traffic.

00:09:04.846 --> 00:09:07.670
Another challenge
and a perception is

00:09:07.671 --> 00:09:09.730
if you're making all these
changes so quickly,

00:09:09.731 --> 00:09:12.830
what happens to
privacy and security?

00:09:12.831 --> 00:09:15.170
A couple of things
to say about that.

00:09:15.171 --> 00:09:16.490
First is organizational.

00:09:16.491 --> 00:09:19.290
The organizational is
really on this slide.

00:09:19.291 --> 00:09:22.665
So in many organizations,
those security people,

00:09:22.666 --> 00:09:26.770
they're in a separate group
and they're not integrated.

00:09:26.771 --> 00:09:28.805
Another strategy is

00:09:28.806 --> 00:09:32.260
SecDevOps where now
it's not just DevOps

00:09:32.261 --> 00:09:33.620
but the security people are in

00:09:33.621 --> 00:09:37.530
there as part of the team as
well and tightly integrated.

00:09:37.531 --> 00:09:41.365
So, one, from an
observational standpoint,

00:09:41.366 --> 00:09:43.360
you would think these continuous

00:09:43.361 --> 00:09:44.580
deployment companies might

00:09:44.581 --> 00:09:47.554
be on the leader of the
DevSecOps movement,

00:09:47.555 --> 00:09:49.310
but really not so.

00:09:49.311 --> 00:09:51.380
There's not a lot
of evidence of them

00:09:51.381 --> 00:09:54.815
having security
integrated as tightly.

00:09:54.816 --> 00:09:58.090
So, if you take it from
a representative standpoint,

00:09:58.091 --> 00:10:00.510
probably DevSecOps is
more of an idea at

00:10:00.511 --> 00:10:04.340
this point than really implemented
in a lot of companies.

00:10:04.341 --> 00:10:06.340
One change that we've seen over

00:10:06.341 --> 00:10:09.135
the four years of the Summit

00:10:09.136 --> 00:10:14.230
is that the security people
are being more integrated,

00:10:14.231 --> 00:10:16.940
that they are being
more partnered

00:10:16.941 --> 00:10:20.035
with as opposed to as
early in the years,

00:10:20.036 --> 00:10:22.060
it was more like
those security people

00:10:22.061 --> 00:10:24.075
they just stop our releases.

00:10:24.076 --> 00:10:27.490
Now, there's more of a
partnership that's taking place.

00:10:27.491 --> 00:10:30.350
So, that may be representative
of companies as a whole

00:10:30.351 --> 00:10:33.440
as security is being
more of excelled,

00:10:33.441 --> 00:10:37.245
more of a partner and not
just stopping releases.

00:10:37.246 --> 00:10:40.180
So, that's organizational
standpoint.

00:10:40.181 --> 00:10:44.060
The way that the companies

00:10:44.061 --> 00:10:45.340
deal with security and privacy

00:10:45.341 --> 00:10:47.325
changes through some
of them anyway is,

00:10:47.326 --> 00:10:49.610
if a change has a security or

00:10:49.611 --> 00:10:51.890
privacy implication, that again,

00:10:51.891 --> 00:10:53.410
they put a feature toggle around

00:10:53.411 --> 00:10:57.260
that change and it is not
visible to the public

00:10:57.261 --> 00:10:59.870
until the security or
privacy people look at

00:10:59.871 --> 00:11:04.020
that change and okay it and
then they release the flag.

00:11:04.021 --> 00:11:05.775
So, that's the way that they,

00:11:05.776 --> 00:11:08.620
in general, control that.

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:12.160
Another thing that
I would say some of

00:11:12.161 --> 00:11:14.320
the more advanced
companies including

00:11:14.321 --> 00:11:17.020
Bing and Windows as

00:11:17.021 --> 00:11:19.900
you'll see, do feature
experimentation.

00:11:19.901 --> 00:11:22.350
So, feature experimentation is

00:11:22.351 --> 00:11:24.040
where the company are putting

00:11:24.041 --> 00:11:25.930
out potentially
multiple versions of

00:11:25.931 --> 00:11:28.820
the same thing or trying
a new feature and

00:11:28.821 --> 00:11:30.410
trying to get data that

00:11:30.411 --> 00:11:32.420
would say whether
the customers have,

00:11:32.421 --> 00:11:34.940
they like the change or not.

00:11:34.941 --> 00:11:37.950
So, this is a Netflix screen and

00:11:37.951 --> 00:11:39.340
Netflix is a big company

00:11:39.341 --> 00:11:41.205
that does feature
experimentation.

00:11:41.206 --> 00:11:42.870
It's possible that if you and I

00:11:42.871 --> 00:11:44.500
sat down with
our laptops and pulled

00:11:44.501 --> 00:11:47.900
up Netflix at this minute
our screens would be different,

00:11:47.901 --> 00:11:50.350
because they're continuously
trying new things

00:11:50.351 --> 00:11:52.960
out and they're watching
where we hover,

00:11:52.961 --> 00:11:54.440
how long we stay and all

00:11:54.441 --> 00:11:56.340
kinds of things about what we do,

00:11:56.341 --> 00:12:00.870
and then they can keep a
change or not keep a change.

00:12:00.871 --> 00:12:04.130
So, in a lean startup,
pivot or persevere,

00:12:04.131 --> 00:12:06.700
they're collecting data,
instrumenting code,

00:12:06.701 --> 00:12:09.045
collecting data,
making decisions.

00:12:09.046 --> 00:12:12.960
Sounds good, but it's
more complicated.

00:12:12.961 --> 00:12:15.240
So, some of the complications,

00:12:15.241 --> 00:12:17.080
back in the early days
of the Summit,

00:12:17.081 --> 00:12:20.000
we actually wrote an iTripoli
software paper and one of

00:12:20.001 --> 00:12:21.610
the adages we put
in the paper was

00:12:21.611 --> 00:12:23.805
"Every feature is an experiment."

00:12:23.806 --> 00:12:26.000
That's the idealistic view.

00:12:26.001 --> 00:12:27.320
Now it's like, "Well, maybe not

00:12:27.321 --> 00:12:29.065
every feature is an experiment",

00:12:29.066 --> 00:12:31.560
because there's just, the cost of

00:12:31.561 --> 00:12:34.990
instrumenting code and
collecting all the data,

00:12:34.991 --> 00:12:36.810
which could be every change you

00:12:36.811 --> 00:12:39.115
might have 1,000
different metrics,

00:12:39.116 --> 00:12:42.630
and then the use of
guardrail metrics,

00:12:42.631 --> 00:12:44.650
is what Microsoft calls it,

00:12:44.651 --> 00:12:48.260
where if there's something
that hits either

00:12:48.261 --> 00:12:52.070
the high or low to say
some big change has taken place,

00:12:52.071 --> 00:12:55.380
then you have to get
alarmed to think of

00:12:55.381 --> 00:12:59.885
what has that change
done and take it out.

00:12:59.886 --> 00:13:02.770
The experiment, even
for companies large

00:13:02.771 --> 00:13:05.735
like Google or Netflix that
have many many customers,

00:13:05.736 --> 00:13:09.260
they can't just run it to get
a good enough sample size,

00:13:09.261 --> 00:13:10.670
because we behave differently

00:13:10.671 --> 00:13:12.380
in the morning than
we do at night.

00:13:12.381 --> 00:13:15.580
We behave differently on
a Monday than we do on a Sunday.

00:13:15.581 --> 00:13:18.230
So, they have to have
longer periods of

00:13:18.231 --> 00:13:21.385
time where they're testing
these features out.

00:13:21.386 --> 00:13:23.950
So, in general,
experimentation is

00:13:23.951 --> 00:13:27.275
more complicated than it was
originally thought about.

00:13:27.276 --> 00:13:29.255
So, with all that,

00:13:29.256 --> 00:13:31.500
the experimentation
has feature toggles,

00:13:31.501 --> 00:13:34.440
architecture database changes
have feature toggles,

00:13:34.441 --> 00:13:36.470
security has feature toggles,

00:13:36.471 --> 00:13:39.355
there's other types
of feature toggles.

00:13:39.356 --> 00:13:43.099
The companies do admit that

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:46.310
when you have these experiments,

00:13:46.311 --> 00:13:47.880
if it's a failed experiment,

00:13:47.881 --> 00:13:49.860
say no, don't make that change,

00:13:49.861 --> 00:13:52.660
they might just take
that code and turn

00:13:52.661 --> 00:13:55.980
the flag off and
leave the code there.

00:13:55.981 --> 00:13:58.090
So, now it's dead code,

00:13:58.091 --> 00:14:00.550
if you will, that's
hanging around.

00:14:00.551 --> 00:14:04.570
So, just all of the code that is

00:14:04.571 --> 00:14:06.250
hidden behind feature toggles can

00:14:06.251 --> 00:14:08.715
remain there causing
technical debt.

00:14:08.716 --> 00:14:13.280
So, you can't have a movement
underway, potentially,

00:14:13.281 --> 00:14:15.385
to identify these
areas of the code

00:14:15.386 --> 00:14:18.180
that have this dead code,

00:14:18.181 --> 00:14:20.155
essentially, and
try to remove it.

00:14:20.156 --> 00:14:22.230
They can cause a problem
because someone

00:14:22.231 --> 00:14:24.220
might see some code
there and say,

00:14:24.221 --> 00:14:25.555
"I like that code",

00:14:25.556 --> 00:14:26.930
but because it's behind

00:14:26.931 --> 00:14:28.550
a feature toggle they're
not sure if they

00:14:28.551 --> 00:14:31.880
can depend on that code or
not depend upon that code.

00:14:31.881 --> 00:14:34.100
So, in general, the complexity

00:14:34.101 --> 00:14:36.240
that's added with all of
these feature toggles,

00:14:36.241 --> 00:14:40.460
the complexity and technical
debt needs to be considered.

00:14:40.461 --> 00:14:44.465
Some radically
different types of testing.

00:14:44.466 --> 00:14:48.520
So, as I mentioned earlier,

00:14:48.521 --> 00:14:52.285
unit testing is done
much more often,

00:14:52.286 --> 00:14:54.275
which is a good thing,

00:14:54.276 --> 00:14:56.190
but there are other
testing strategies.

00:14:56.191 --> 00:14:58.645
So, testing strategies
would say that,

00:14:58.646 --> 00:15:01.050
"You put this code down
in production behind

00:15:01.051 --> 00:15:03.810
a feature flag and you
test it through the API."

00:15:03.811 --> 00:15:05.260
So, when I think about what we

00:15:05.261 --> 00:15:06.830
teach students at university,

00:15:06.831 --> 00:15:09.000
we're not teaching them about
doing something like that.

00:15:09.001 --> 00:15:10.620
We're teaching them about deploy

00:15:10.621 --> 00:15:13.755
your code and then
test it in production.

00:15:13.756 --> 00:15:16.330
Some companies say
they have to test in

00:15:16.331 --> 00:15:18.760
production because they
need that level of scale,

00:15:18.761 --> 00:15:20.550
they need to be on

00:15:20.551 --> 00:15:22.150
production servers to make

00:15:22.151 --> 00:15:23.680
sure that it's working properly.

00:15:23.681 --> 00:15:25.640
So, deploy it out there through

00:15:25.641 --> 00:15:27.400
a dark launch so the users

00:15:27.401 --> 00:15:29.650
can't see it and
test it that way,

00:15:29.651 --> 00:15:31.890
but then the next
level of testing

00:15:31.891 --> 00:15:34.060
is as you're deploying
the changes.

00:15:34.061 --> 00:15:37.430
So, continuous deployment
companies typically may dog

00:15:37.431 --> 00:15:41.060
food a change so it's only
visible to own employees,

00:15:41.061 --> 00:15:43.970
and then when that works,

00:15:43.971 --> 00:15:47.140
then deploy it to a 0.5
percent of the population,

00:15:47.141 --> 00:15:48.380
one percent of the population,

00:15:48.381 --> 00:15:49.570
five percent of the population.

00:15:49.571 --> 00:15:52.280
You're watching to
see how it reacts,

00:15:52.281 --> 00:15:54.420
which that's a form of
testing in production.

00:15:54.421 --> 00:15:58.895
You're looking at the types
of changes and defects,

00:15:58.896 --> 00:16:02.030
and pulling back potentially or

00:16:02.031 --> 00:16:03.140
rolling a new version out

00:16:03.141 --> 00:16:06.210
quickly to take care of a change.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:10.940
Mobile deployment is
definitely a challenge.

00:16:10.941 --> 00:16:14.300
So, they can even say,
"Web development,

00:16:14.301 --> 00:16:17.425
we got that one", but mobile
deployment is harder.

00:16:17.426 --> 00:16:20.880
The Play Stores, they do

00:16:20.881 --> 00:16:24.610
not allow you to
continuously deploy changes.

00:16:24.611 --> 00:16:26.810
A company may only be able

00:16:26.811 --> 00:16:29.650
to make a new
version once a week.

00:16:29.651 --> 00:16:32.550
So you can't have
the same type of strategy.

00:16:32.551 --> 00:16:34.610
So, they have to be
more careful with

00:16:34.611 --> 00:16:37.260
their quality for
their mobile deployment.

00:16:37.261 --> 00:16:40.460
Also, they have to consider
that on mobile devices,

00:16:40.461 --> 00:16:42.160
you may take a version and you

00:16:42.161 --> 00:16:44.200
may never take another version.

00:16:44.201 --> 00:16:45.690
So, there can be

00:16:45.691 --> 00:16:47.580
an infinite amount of
different versions

00:16:47.581 --> 00:16:49.075
out on the customers.

00:16:49.076 --> 00:16:52.430
I mean, they even
talked about TVs,

00:16:52.431 --> 00:16:54.340
said it would be
cheaper, Netflix says,

00:16:54.341 --> 00:16:57.730
"It would be cheaper for
us to buy all the TVs back

00:16:57.731 --> 00:16:59.130
with some of the versions on

00:16:59.131 --> 00:17:01.870
them than to maintain
those versions."

00:17:01.871 --> 00:17:04.180
So, there was this change in

00:17:04.181 --> 00:17:07.030
this year where people
were talking about apps

00:17:07.031 --> 00:17:11.069
being built with
a much thinner Client

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:14.330
and more JavaScript, more code,

00:17:14.331 --> 00:17:16.320
so that what's on the phone or

00:17:16.321 --> 00:17:17.810
on the mobile device itself is

00:17:17.811 --> 00:17:20.590
smaller and everything
else being on

00:17:20.591 --> 00:17:23.815
a JavaScript or on the server,

00:17:23.816 --> 00:17:28.410
and the feature flags being
able to be changed so that

00:17:28.411 --> 00:17:30.430
you don't actually have
to deploy a new version

00:17:30.431 --> 00:17:32.710
on the phone or on
the mobile device,

00:17:32.711 --> 00:17:34.480
you just change the feature flags

00:17:34.481 --> 00:17:36.480
centrally in order to take

00:17:36.481 --> 00:17:39.070
care of some of these concerns.

00:17:39.071 --> 00:17:41.695
Then the last one
is tool investment.

00:17:41.696 --> 00:17:43.940
There's a lot of focus on tools,

00:17:43.941 --> 00:17:49.020
open-source tools to control
the deployment pipeline.

00:17:49.021 --> 00:17:51.490
There's Chaos Monkey tool where

00:17:51.491 --> 00:17:55.030
there are companies,
particularly Netflix,

00:17:55.031 --> 00:17:59.270
are injecting instability into

00:17:59.271 --> 00:18:00.310
the network to see what would

00:18:00.311 --> 00:18:04.080
happen and the companies
share these types of tools,

00:18:04.081 --> 00:18:05.510
but yet all of the companies have

00:18:05.511 --> 00:18:07.725
large IT organizations as well,

00:18:07.726 --> 00:18:10.665
in order to support
continuous deployment.

00:18:10.666 --> 00:18:14.020
The whole pipeline, the
automated unit tests,

00:18:14.021 --> 00:18:16.190
the automated static analysis,

00:18:16.191 --> 00:18:18.950
any fuzzing, those are tools,

00:18:18.951 --> 00:18:20.980
I mean that from a
research standpoint,

00:18:20.981 --> 00:18:23.440
the companies are really
looking for good tools

00:18:23.441 --> 00:18:26.230
and management of false positive,

00:18:26.231 --> 00:18:27.720
that type of thing so
they can put it in

00:18:27.721 --> 00:18:31.710
the tool chain and not
need human intervention.

00:18:31.711 --> 00:18:33.010
So, from a research standpoint,

00:18:33.011 --> 00:18:34.995
there's a lot of need there.

00:18:34.996 --> 00:18:38.085
Then the last one, Culture Shift.

00:18:38.086 --> 00:18:40.050
Culture shift in that now

00:18:40.051 --> 00:18:42.310
the developers are the ones
that are typically

00:18:42.311 --> 00:18:46.415
called when a problem surfaces,

00:18:46.416 --> 00:18:47.530
where before it used to be like

00:18:47.531 --> 00:18:50.140
those support people
handle the field problems.

00:18:50.141 --> 00:18:51.820
Now, it's usually, "I

00:18:51.821 --> 00:18:53.975
integrated something today,
it caused a problem.

00:18:53.976 --> 00:18:55.730
I need to deal with it," which

00:18:55.731 --> 00:18:57.635
means the developers
seem to be much

00:18:57.636 --> 00:18:59.885
more cognizant of their quality

00:18:59.886 --> 00:19:01.730
and putting out
high quality products,

00:19:01.731 --> 00:19:03.305
which is a good thing.

00:19:03.306 --> 00:19:05.150
But on the other hand, it adds

00:19:05.151 --> 00:19:07.330
another level of stress
they didn't used to have,

00:19:07.331 --> 00:19:09.075
which is not always a good thing.

00:19:09.076 --> 00:19:10.710
Then, just in general,

00:19:10.711 --> 00:19:12.320
the new types of development

00:19:12.321 --> 00:19:14.085
that happen with
continuous deployment,

00:19:14.086 --> 00:19:17.290
it just causes
a huge culture change,

00:19:17.291 --> 00:19:19.355
which not all of
the people say it's,

00:19:19.356 --> 00:19:22.080
they actually say it's
the toughest thing of all.

00:19:22.081 --> 00:19:24.500
It is the big culture change
because of all of

00:19:24.501 --> 00:19:25.760
the different things that

00:19:25.761 --> 00:19:28.905
the developers have to do
that they didn't used to do.

00:19:28.906 --> 00:19:31.970
They talked about having
shows where they go from

00:19:31.971 --> 00:19:35.440
location to location talking
to the development team,

00:19:35.441 --> 00:19:39.410
sending someone with a success
story to another location.

00:19:39.411 --> 00:19:42.540
VMware, they talked
about going from,

00:19:42.541 --> 00:19:44.540
in this journey, going from

00:19:44.541 --> 00:19:46.710
multiple branches to one branch.

00:19:46.711 --> 00:19:49.220
So, they killed
the branches and they

00:19:49.221 --> 00:19:51.620
had some ceremony where
they had cupcakes

00:19:51.621 --> 00:19:54.230
with coffins on them
and brought them to

00:19:54.231 --> 00:19:57.020
all the locations for a ceremony

00:19:57.021 --> 00:19:59.850
for killing the branches to
make lighthearted of it.

00:19:59.851 --> 00:20:03.295
But they say the culture changes
are actually quite hard.

00:20:03.296 --> 00:20:06.130
To summarize, lots of changes in

00:20:06.131 --> 00:20:08.700
a way changes everything.

00:20:08.701 --> 00:20:10.490
But there's a lot
of opportunity in

00:20:10.491 --> 00:20:13.320
those changes along
with the challenges.

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:23.045
&gt;&gt; So, one of the things
in the summary is that

00:20:23.046 --> 00:20:24.730
most people have

00:20:24.731 --> 00:20:28.415
very similar problems
because it's difficult.

00:20:28.416 --> 00:20:31.185
When you have large software,

00:20:31.186 --> 00:20:33.050
it's just difficult to have

00:20:33.051 --> 00:20:35.090
to change it with
so many different people.

00:20:35.091 --> 00:20:39.230
So, Bosses say is having
that range of different types

00:20:39.231 --> 00:20:42.930
of Product types is
quite useful to learn

00:20:42.931 --> 00:20:44.750
how effective CD is

00:20:44.751 --> 00:20:46.920
and how ineffective
is if at a time so,

00:20:46.921 --> 00:20:50.175
Bing has a different perspective
on this. Chap, join in.

00:20:50.176 --> 00:20:54.800
&gt;&gt; Hello everybody.
So, I'm from Bing,

00:20:54.801 --> 00:20:56.990
we're going to be talking
specifically about Bing UX,

00:20:56.991 --> 00:20:58.520
so if you've ever gone to Bing,

00:20:58.521 --> 00:21:00.225
thank you for your support.

00:21:00.226 --> 00:21:02.170
Everything you see
is built on top of

00:21:02.171 --> 00:21:03.520
the platform that we're

00:21:03.521 --> 00:21:05.160
going to discuss
a little bit here today.

00:21:05.161 --> 00:21:11.135
So, some of the core
Bing wide principles;

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:13.815
Life-site quality is paramount.

00:21:13.816 --> 00:21:17.660
We were our brand is the quality.

00:21:17.661 --> 00:21:19.610
If we have bad quality,

00:21:19.611 --> 00:21:20.985
people will go elsewhere.

00:21:20.986 --> 00:21:23.695
We're a second place
in a difficult race,

00:21:23.696 --> 00:21:26.040
so everything we do has
to be high-quality.

00:21:26.041 --> 00:21:30.120
We always are looking to
innovate and produce new things.

00:21:30.121 --> 00:21:31.940
Again, it's a competition

00:21:31.941 --> 00:21:34.525
and we need to make sure
that we're keeping pace,

00:21:34.526 --> 00:21:36.750
we need to find
differentiators that

00:21:36.751 --> 00:21:40.070
draw people toward
us. Data rules.

00:21:40.071 --> 00:21:43.605
So, some of the things that
Laurae was just talking about,

00:21:43.606 --> 00:21:45.310
you're going to see echoes

00:21:45.311 --> 00:21:47.450
of it, it's really cool actually.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:50.090
Data is what we use,

00:21:50.091 --> 00:21:51.520
we can't use emotion because if

00:21:51.521 --> 00:21:54.100
we're automating everything,
there's no emotion.

00:21:54.101 --> 00:21:55.970
So, we have to make sure
that we pay attention to

00:21:55.971 --> 00:21:57.410
the data and we have to believe

00:21:57.411 --> 00:21:59.285
the data and we have
to use the data.

00:21:59.286 --> 00:22:03.370
Search is an expensive business,
it's complicated,

00:22:03.371 --> 00:22:05.350
it's very expensive
to run and so,

00:22:05.351 --> 00:22:06.360
everything that we can do to

00:22:06.361 --> 00:22:08.020
improve it to make things
a little bit better,

00:22:08.021 --> 00:22:09.280
a little bit faster, a little bit

00:22:09.281 --> 00:22:11.775
more automated is what we go for.

00:22:11.776 --> 00:22:16.255
So, here's a high level of
the Bing serving stack.

00:22:16.256 --> 00:22:18.760
It's a multi-tier stack.

00:22:18.761 --> 00:22:21.215
They are hosted in servers,

00:22:21.216 --> 00:22:24.115
do distributed
around the world and

00:22:24.116 --> 00:22:27.290
each tier is on its own
deployment cadence,

00:22:27.291 --> 00:22:29.275
as well as their own codebase.

00:22:29.276 --> 00:22:32.945
We handle ingress,
routing, and throttling,

00:22:32.946 --> 00:22:34.615
and then the UX rendering tier,

00:22:34.616 --> 00:22:36.750
data query and
aggregation and then the

00:22:36.751 --> 00:22:40.540
various data-based indexes
at the bottom.

00:22:40.541 --> 00:22:45.375
A vertical along that side
is metrics and monitoring.

00:22:45.376 --> 00:22:49.340
We're talking today about
the UX data presentation tier.

00:22:49.341 --> 00:22:51.120
We have many hundreds
of developers

00:22:51.121 --> 00:22:53.040
in this single branch.

00:22:53.041 --> 00:22:55.050
We're seeing an
exponential growth over

00:22:55.051 --> 00:22:57.160
the last 5-7 years,

00:22:57.161 --> 00:23:01.160
precisely because of the success
we've had with improving

00:23:01.161 --> 00:23:03.160
Agility and continuous
integration

00:23:03.161 --> 00:23:05.425
and continuous delivery.

00:23:05.426 --> 00:23:08.250
The UX data and
presentation tiered Bing is

00:23:08.251 --> 00:23:10.370
roughly is less than
100 milliseconds

00:23:10.371 --> 00:23:12.200
at the 95th overall,

00:23:12.201 --> 00:23:14.695
and we render all of the HTML,

00:23:14.696 --> 00:23:16.400
we handle the API entry points,

00:23:16.401 --> 00:23:20.440
we handle Cortana, handle Yahoo.

00:23:20.441 --> 00:23:22.650
There's other deals
with Yahoo and Apple.

00:23:22.651 --> 00:23:25.330
So, we just recover
a large space,

00:23:25.331 --> 00:23:27.570
is very large monolith.

00:23:28.490 --> 00:23:30.750
So, why did Bing
invest in Agility?

00:23:30.751 --> 00:23:32.890
This is what I was
just talking about.

00:23:32.891 --> 00:23:35.730
We need to stay competitive.

00:23:35.731 --> 00:23:37.345
It's a question of survival.

00:23:37.346 --> 00:23:39.040
If we can't keep pace,

00:23:39.041 --> 00:23:40.140
if we can't differentiate and

00:23:40.141 --> 00:23:42.290
innovate faster
than our opponent,

00:23:42.291 --> 00:23:44.535
then we're going to continue
to fall by the wayside,

00:23:44.536 --> 00:23:45.825
people will just abandon us.

00:23:45.826 --> 00:23:47.685
So, I want to keep my job,

00:23:47.686 --> 00:23:49.070
I need to support my children,

00:23:49.071 --> 00:23:51.285
so I was trying to
do what we can.

00:23:51.286 --> 00:23:53.180
We need to make sure
that we're able to

00:23:53.181 --> 00:23:54.910
capture more market share.

00:23:54.911 --> 00:23:56.400
We run a lot of experiments and

00:23:56.401 --> 00:23:57.980
this is another thing
Laurie was talking about,

00:23:57.981 --> 00:23:59.665
the emphasis on experimentation,

00:23:59.666 --> 00:24:00.945
we run a lot of experience,

00:24:00.946 --> 00:24:02.490
thousands of them per month.

00:24:02.491 --> 00:24:03.900
Each tier that you saw in

00:24:03.901 --> 00:24:07.110
the previous slides
is capable of running

00:24:07.111 --> 00:24:09.565
their own experiments and
so there's a lot of things

00:24:09.566 --> 00:24:12.990
going on the Bing
stack every day.

00:24:12.991 --> 00:24:14.880
So, the question then becomes,

00:24:14.881 --> 00:24:17.080
how can we run more experiments?

00:24:17.081 --> 00:24:18.885
We need to see
a dramatic increase

00:24:18.886 --> 00:24:21.075
in developer productivity.

00:24:21.076 --> 00:24:22.570
We need to figure out
a way to make sure that

00:24:22.571 --> 00:24:24.265
they are further desk,

00:24:24.266 --> 00:24:25.430
getting stuff done, fingers on

00:24:25.431 --> 00:24:27.935
the keyboard, making commits.

00:24:27.936 --> 00:24:29.770
We need a dramatic increase

00:24:29.771 --> 00:24:32.070
in the number of
developers as well.

00:24:32.071 --> 00:24:34.860
We've only got five people
who are working on Bing,

00:24:34.861 --> 00:24:38.090
we're not going to see
a high pace of innovation,

00:24:38.091 --> 00:24:40.520
so we need to get
more people working on it.

00:24:40.521 --> 00:24:41.650
Finally, we need to see

00:24:41.651 --> 00:24:44.705
a dramatic increase
in experimentation.

00:24:44.706 --> 00:24:47.170
So, we need to make sure
that these features,

00:24:47.171 --> 00:24:49.025
these experiments
that you're doing are

00:24:49.026 --> 00:24:51.420
easily done, easily created,

00:24:51.421 --> 00:24:54.600
easily implemented,
easily experiments

00:24:54.601 --> 00:24:56.420
are seen in production and then

00:24:56.421 --> 00:24:59.440
the data analysis needs
to be simple as well.

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:04.640
So, let's fast-forward five
years, what was the impact?

00:25:04.641 --> 00:25:05.940
What was the impact of Agility

00:25:05.941 --> 00:25:07.685
and specifically
the Bing UX Agility?

00:25:07.686 --> 00:25:10.250
We saw a dramatic increase
in the quality

00:25:10.251 --> 00:25:11.580
of the live-site and
this is something

00:25:11.581 --> 00:25:12.945
that Laurie was saying as well.

00:25:12.946 --> 00:25:14.785
It was almost overnight.

00:25:14.786 --> 00:25:16.270
So, we were previously doing

00:25:16.271 --> 00:25:18.600
monthly releases which really

00:25:18.601 --> 00:25:20.720
not until like every 4-6 weeks.

00:25:20.721 --> 00:25:21.590
We wanted it to be

00:25:21.591 --> 00:25:24.100
every four weeks but
what would happen is,

00:25:24.101 --> 00:25:26.160
we would deploy to the live-site.

00:25:26.161 --> 00:25:28.200
There'd be a bunch of things
broken on the live-site,

00:25:28.201 --> 00:25:29.220
we'd spend the next week or

00:25:29.221 --> 00:25:32.205
two weeks hot fixing them and so,

00:25:32.206 --> 00:25:33.935
we always see this delay.

00:25:33.936 --> 00:25:36.270
When we switched over to Agility,

00:25:36.271 --> 00:25:37.850
it was really impressive,

00:25:37.851 --> 00:25:40.440
we went from six or more
live-site incidence per

00:25:40.441 --> 00:25:43.810
month to less than
one on average.

00:25:43.811 --> 00:25:46.625
We saw that there was an impact
in engineering as well.

00:25:46.626 --> 00:25:48.665
The developers are
much more satisfied.

00:25:48.666 --> 00:25:52.175
They can see more quickly
the result of their work.

00:25:52.176 --> 00:25:53.920
It's no longer
an asynchronous staying

00:25:53.921 --> 00:25:56.255
where they check in
something on day one,

00:25:56.256 --> 00:25:57.630
it goes out on day 30.

00:25:57.631 --> 00:26:00.410
For us, they check in on
something on day one and we

00:26:00.411 --> 00:26:01.630
sometimes even get it out on

00:26:01.631 --> 00:26:03.815
day one but at the worst
we'll get it out on day two,

00:26:03.816 --> 00:26:07.210
and so they have
a very quick feedback loop.

00:26:07.211 --> 00:26:09.520
Our pipeline has
also been able to

00:26:09.521 --> 00:26:12.475
scale to meet this new demand.

00:26:12.476 --> 00:26:13.910
We've actually done a lot of

00:26:13.911 --> 00:26:16.330
optimizations to
continue to support

00:26:16.331 --> 00:26:20.370
that based on capacity and
resourcing issues that we had.

00:26:20.371 --> 00:26:23.620
We also see an impact on
the code base because people

00:26:23.621 --> 00:26:25.075
or the developers now are

00:26:25.076 --> 00:26:26.905
able to get things
done more quickly.

00:26:26.906 --> 00:26:30.450
We see much more
smaller code reviews.

00:26:30.451 --> 00:26:32.060
If you're reviewing less code,

00:26:32.061 --> 00:26:33.405
you're able to do a better job.

00:26:33.406 --> 00:26:34.610
There's fewer files to look

00:26:34.611 --> 00:26:36.160
at and fewer changes in each one,

00:26:36.161 --> 00:26:39.175
you get a much better
overall code review

00:26:39.176 --> 00:26:41.160
than you would for
larger changes.

00:26:41.161 --> 00:26:42.810
They're also shorter and more

00:26:42.811 --> 00:26:46.400
focused and last but not least,

00:26:46.401 --> 00:26:48.110
we've empowered everyone,

00:26:48.111 --> 00:26:49.935
Laurie was talking
about culture as well.

00:26:49.936 --> 00:26:51.260
We empower everyone to

00:26:51.261 --> 00:26:52.890
reject a code review
that doesn't have

00:26:52.891 --> 00:26:54.450
tests because the tests

00:26:54.451 --> 00:26:56.270
are what we live and
die of the Agility.

00:26:56.271 --> 00:26:58.485
This is something Laurie
was saying over and over,

00:26:58.486 --> 00:27:01.010
it's the tests that
make the difference.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.320
So, here are some of
the pre-Agility metrics,

00:27:04.321 --> 00:27:08.915
this would be from
roughly 2011 or '12.

00:27:08.916 --> 00:27:12.325
We had roughly 80 engineers
in our code base.

00:27:12.326 --> 00:27:14.160
Some of the more
interesting thing is we had

00:27:14.161 --> 00:27:17.050
five different development repos;

00:27:17.051 --> 00:27:19.760
one main repo and
a separate release repo.

00:27:19.761 --> 00:27:22.790
So, you can imagine
the reverse integrations and

00:27:22.791 --> 00:27:23.660
the forward integrations we

00:27:23.661 --> 00:27:24.780
tend to do every month
and it would

00:27:24.781 --> 00:27:27.835
be a huge delay to try to
get that stuff sorted out.

00:27:27.836 --> 00:27:30.035
We were able to ship
once per month.

00:27:30.036 --> 00:27:32.320
We had roughly 3,000 tests,

00:27:32.321 --> 00:27:36.610
80 percent of which would pass
and they would run ad-hoc.

00:27:36.611 --> 00:27:38.720
So, what I've said
in the past is,

00:27:38.721 --> 00:27:41.785
we had some tests,
some of which run.

00:27:41.786 --> 00:27:43.255
Of those tests that ran,

00:27:43.256 --> 00:27:45.210
some of them passed and it was

00:27:45.211 --> 00:27:48.170
just a giant question
mark for everything else.

00:27:48.171 --> 00:27:51.200
We in terms of the test
tasks that we're able to do,

00:27:51.201 --> 00:27:53.100
remember I'm talking
about the Bing UX,

00:27:53.101 --> 00:27:55.535
we had less than three browsers.

00:27:55.536 --> 00:27:56.950
It was whatever
the developer had on

00:27:56.951 --> 00:27:58.560
their machine if they wanted to

00:27:58.561 --> 00:28:00.465
do any browser-based
testing at all.

00:28:00.466 --> 00:28:02.530
We handled the Bing
Web results and we

00:28:02.531 --> 00:28:04.560
would see roughly 10
experiments per month.

00:28:04.561 --> 00:28:06.600
Now think about, it

00:28:06.601 --> 00:28:08.910
is like it was a choose
your own adventure.

00:28:08.911 --> 00:28:11.970
If you wanted to do
effective experimentation

00:28:11.971 --> 00:28:14.110
at Bing at that time,
you'd have to say, "Okay,

00:28:14.111 --> 00:28:16.390
I'm going to try
this experiment where I change

00:28:16.391 --> 00:28:19.230
the link color to purple
and if that one works,

00:28:19.231 --> 00:28:20.450
I think I'm going
to try to underline

00:28:20.451 --> 00:28:21.540
it but if that one doesn't work,

00:28:21.541 --> 00:28:22.670
I think we're going
to try to bold

00:28:22.671 --> 00:28:24.190
it and if the bold one
doesn't work,

00:28:24.191 --> 00:28:25.965
I think I'm going try
to do this and that."

00:28:25.966 --> 00:28:27.800
They would have to do all
of that coding ahead of

00:28:27.801 --> 00:28:30.385
time because we only
shipped once a month.

00:28:30.386 --> 00:28:31.630
They'd have that
one shot, they get

00:28:31.631 --> 00:28:32.790
the code in and then they could

00:28:32.791 --> 00:28:34.310
experiment and so they always

00:28:34.311 --> 00:28:36.085
had to do much more
work than they needed.

00:28:36.086 --> 00:28:39.060
So, that's why we saw
so few experiments.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.070
So, here we are today.

00:28:42.071 --> 00:28:47.195
Here we are about when I wrote
this deck, it's 46th now.

00:28:47.196 --> 00:28:51.220
We seen over a tenfold increase
in the number of

00:28:51.221 --> 00:28:53.455
developers working our branch

00:28:53.456 --> 00:28:56.130
and it's basically
because like I said,

00:28:56.131 --> 00:28:57.425
they've seen the success,

00:28:57.426 --> 00:29:01.230
they've all onboarded
to our repo and so,

00:29:01.231 --> 00:29:04.220
there's one repo that
everyone is working in.

00:29:04.221 --> 00:29:06.310
We see 15-minute builds,

00:29:06.311 --> 00:29:08.325
we see five minutes start times,

00:29:08.326 --> 00:29:10.975
and it's 10 minutes to test now,

00:29:10.976 --> 00:29:13.715
which was 20 and met
our goal finally.

00:29:13.716 --> 00:29:15.640
We have one GIT repo,

00:29:15.641 --> 00:29:18.240
it's a mono repo, everyone's
checking into it.

00:29:18.241 --> 00:29:21.430
We deliver code 14 times a week,

00:29:21.431 --> 00:29:23.660
in fact last week
it was 17 times.

00:29:23.661 --> 00:29:26.220
We have 46,000 tests now.

00:29:26.221 --> 00:29:28.660
We have four nines and
the tests pass and

00:29:28.661 --> 00:29:32.250
auto execution with 14 browsers,

00:29:32.251 --> 00:29:36.590
34 devices so you see that
we've been able to get

00:29:36.591 --> 00:29:38.900
things done and scale them well

00:29:38.901 --> 00:29:41.515
so that we are continuing
to improve things.

00:29:41.516 --> 00:29:44.785
Looking at the inner
and outer loops,

00:29:44.786 --> 00:29:46.910
the continuous integration,
continuous delivery,

00:29:46.911 --> 00:29:49.690
both highly integrated
with Visual Studio.

00:29:49.691 --> 00:29:50.970
In fact, my goal is to

00:29:50.971 --> 00:29:53.000
completely integrate
with Visual Studio so

00:29:53.001 --> 00:29:56.505
that they have one screen
that they look at all day.

00:29:56.506 --> 00:29:59.435
Right now there's
still some variation.

00:29:59.436 --> 00:30:02.430
Build and test, deploy staging

00:30:02.431 --> 00:30:05.320
and we ship gated by
a 100 percent pass rate.

00:30:05.321 --> 00:30:07.550
We test everything up front.

00:30:07.551 --> 00:30:09.720
All those 46,000 tests are run at

00:30:09.721 --> 00:30:10.780
code review time and if

00:30:10.781 --> 00:30:13.780
any test fails, they
don't get checked in.

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:16.190
Our Staged deployments
and we were

00:30:16.191 --> 00:30:17.845
talking about staging as well,

00:30:17.846 --> 00:30:20.780
we deployed to
a canary environment first,

00:30:20.781 --> 00:30:22.385
scale unit by skill unit.

00:30:22.386 --> 00:30:26.490
If that passes, then we
deliver the rest in parallel.

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:31.060
So, one of the things that we've

00:30:31.061 --> 00:30:33.900
learned is that we
just break stuff,

00:30:33.901 --> 00:30:37.330
we broke Visual Studio
for a while.

00:30:37.331 --> 00:30:40.105
They've broken us multiple times.

00:30:40.106 --> 00:30:43.670
What's nice though is
that our scale actually

00:30:43.671 --> 00:30:47.840
determines whether
other partners can scale or not.

00:30:47.841 --> 00:30:49.660
But in doing so it
helps to improve

00:30:49.661 --> 00:30:52.170
them and them improving
it helps to improve us.

00:30:52.171 --> 00:30:54.000
So, we had a really
good relationship with

00:30:54.001 --> 00:30:55.905
our Engineering Excellence Team,

00:30:55.906 --> 00:30:56.890
really good relationship with

00:30:56.891 --> 00:30:59.410
the Visual Studio Team Services

00:30:59.411 --> 00:31:02.005
and any of the other partners
that we work with.

00:31:02.006 --> 00:31:04.135
We improve everything
that we can.

00:31:04.136 --> 00:31:06.755
My team is not afraid to jump

00:31:06.756 --> 00:31:08.540
organizational boundaries or jump

00:31:08.541 --> 00:31:10.485
to another repo or
to make a change,

00:31:10.486 --> 00:31:12.250
to improve it
because we know that

00:31:12.251 --> 00:31:14.185
the change improves
it for everybody

00:31:14.186 --> 00:31:16.350
else and they're fully

00:31:16.351 --> 00:31:18.065
empowered to do
whatever they need.

00:31:18.066 --> 00:31:20.080
We know also that scale

00:31:20.081 --> 00:31:24.180
magnifies improvements
and regressions.

00:31:24.181 --> 00:31:26.650
Great example of that is we

00:31:26.651 --> 00:31:29.450
run seven percent of
our tests on the browser.

00:31:29.451 --> 00:31:32.505
So the test starts and says,

00:31:32.506 --> 00:31:34.220
startup this browser, waits for

00:31:34.221 --> 00:31:36.310
the browser to start up
and it runs the test.

00:31:36.311 --> 00:31:37.865
That's inefficient right?

00:31:37.866 --> 00:31:40.300
If it takes four seconds to
start the browser and we have

00:31:40.301 --> 00:31:44.380
46,000 tests then we've
wasted minutes of time.

00:31:44.381 --> 00:31:45.960
So, what we've done
is we pre-warmed

00:31:45.961 --> 00:31:48.125
the browsers and we have
the browsers already running,

00:31:48.126 --> 00:31:49.990
and it tests grabs the next one
and runs it, right?

00:31:49.991 --> 00:31:51.870
So, we just find that
we can continue to

00:31:51.871 --> 00:31:55.105
improve just because
we want to get faster.

00:31:55.106 --> 00:31:57.675
And we play, yes, we
play well with others.

00:31:57.676 --> 00:32:02.130
We also got something
coming up with.Net Core.

00:32:02.131 --> 00:32:03.800
It's going to be really exciting.

00:32:03.801 --> 00:32:06.070
So we decided and we

00:32:06.071 --> 00:32:08.745
learned that if you have
fewer moving parts,

00:32:08.746 --> 00:32:10.475
again this is some,

00:32:10.476 --> 00:32:12.985
what I been saying, if you
have fewer moving parts,

00:32:12.986 --> 00:32:14.265
less things can break,

00:32:14.266 --> 00:32:16.245
you have much better
standardization

00:32:16.246 --> 00:32:18.210
and things run much
more smoothly.

00:32:18.211 --> 00:32:21.080
We have one environment to
monitor, that's production.

00:32:21.081 --> 00:32:23.700
Even our test environment
is production,

00:32:23.701 --> 00:32:25.580
just doesn't take traffic.

00:32:25.581 --> 00:32:27.310
And we see that there's

00:32:27.311 --> 00:32:28.920
one validation gate,
so like I said,

00:32:28.921 --> 00:32:31.575
"If you are trying to check in,

00:32:31.576 --> 00:32:32.900
we're going to run all the tests.

00:32:32.901 --> 00:32:34.215
If anyone of them fails

00:32:34.216 --> 00:32:36.695
you are not checking
in until you fix it."

00:32:36.696 --> 00:32:39.465
And all of our tests
are co-located,

00:32:39.466 --> 00:32:41.075
meaning that they're right there.

00:32:41.076 --> 00:32:42.420
So, there's the production code

00:32:42.421 --> 00:32:43.580
and test code right next to it,

00:32:43.581 --> 00:32:45.450
there's no separate repo where

00:32:45.451 --> 00:32:47.480
we store tests definitions
or test results,

00:32:47.481 --> 00:32:50.540
everything is strictly
defined out in our codebase.

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:54.735
Yes. So, how can we optimize
continuous integration?

00:32:54.736 --> 00:32:57.050
We took every optimization
we possibly could

00:32:57.051 --> 00:32:59.115
and we did that over,
and over, and over.

00:32:59.116 --> 00:33:00.480
Everything that we
could find that would

00:33:00.481 --> 00:33:02.125
improve even by a minute,

00:33:02.126 --> 00:33:04.190
our test pass or build

00:33:04.191 --> 00:33:06.250
times is an improvement
that it makes

00:33:06.251 --> 00:33:08.190
for these 900 developers who

00:33:08.191 --> 00:33:11.390
stands here every day and
are able to move faster.

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:16.200
That's the same as it
was, sorry about that.

00:33:16.201 --> 00:33:18.490
We also want to optimize
the outer loop,

00:33:18.491 --> 00:33:21.550
so we have metrics that
tell us how much time we're

00:33:21.551 --> 00:33:23.275
spending in each part
of the outer loop

00:33:23.276 --> 00:33:25.160
so we know what we can improve.

00:33:25.161 --> 00:33:28.705
We're reusing the continuous
integration mechanics.

00:33:28.706 --> 00:33:30.640
What you saw in
the Visual Studio stuff,

00:33:30.641 --> 00:33:32.360
we're reusing Visual Studio
for the outer loop as

00:33:32.361 --> 00:33:34.940
well to handle
the deployments for us.

00:33:34.941 --> 00:33:38.075
We measure all aspects
of continuous delivery.

00:33:38.076 --> 00:33:40.080
If you can't measure something,

00:33:40.081 --> 00:33:42.325
if you can't decide
whether you're improving,

00:33:42.326 --> 00:33:43.660
it's just bad, right?

00:33:43.661 --> 00:33:45.755
So, metrics are super,
super important.

00:33:45.756 --> 00:33:47.695
Finally, we remove humans.

00:33:47.696 --> 00:33:49.045
So, like I said, automation,

00:33:49.046 --> 00:33:50.765
she set it over and
over automation.

00:33:50.766 --> 00:33:52.060
If you remove the humans,

00:33:52.061 --> 00:33:54.730
you will immediately improve
because now you're forcing

00:33:54.731 --> 00:33:58.180
yourself to actually have
operationalized system.

00:33:58.181 --> 00:34:03.150
Okay. So, I think this
is the last slide,

00:34:03.151 --> 00:34:05.190
I'm getting the signal.

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:08.135
This is it, right?

00:34:08.136 --> 00:34:13.075
You implement, then
measure, and then iterate.

00:34:13.076 --> 00:34:15.630
This is us over and over,
this is experimentation.

00:34:15.631 --> 00:34:17.580
We believe in
experimentation so much that

00:34:17.581 --> 00:34:19.915
we actually do it
on our own code.

00:34:19.916 --> 00:34:21.515
All the code that I have,

00:34:21.516 --> 00:34:22.780
all the code that's
been written is

00:34:22.781 --> 00:34:24.300
eventually going to
become obsolete;

00:34:24.301 --> 00:34:25.895
we just assume that's the fact.

00:34:25.896 --> 00:34:27.310
We're always looking to see what

00:34:27.311 --> 00:34:29.150
the next longest pole is,

00:34:29.151 --> 00:34:30.640
how can we improve this while

00:34:30.641 --> 00:34:31.870
this is longer and
we go after it.

00:34:31.871 --> 00:34:34.820
It doesn't matter where
it is, we just chase it.

00:34:38.590 --> 00:34:41.240
Yes, loyalty is a myth.

00:34:41.241 --> 00:34:42.790
I gave this talk

00:34:42.791 --> 00:34:44.330
before and people
weren't happy with that.

00:34:44.331 --> 00:34:46.325
I was in a different country.

00:34:46.326 --> 00:34:51.675
But, a better way of
saying that maybe is,

00:34:51.676 --> 00:34:54.985
they'll go wherever
it makes sense to go.

00:34:54.986 --> 00:34:57.725
There's no loyalty to
the people in my branch.

00:34:57.726 --> 00:34:59.480
If they don't like
what I'm doing or

00:34:59.481 --> 00:35:01.220
if they don't like
the system that I built,

00:35:01.221 --> 00:35:03.305
they'll go elsewhere,
it's not that hard.

00:35:03.306 --> 00:35:05.680
So, we know that
we're successful,

00:35:05.681 --> 00:35:07.290
and we aim to please
those developers

00:35:07.291 --> 00:35:08.760
so that they stay
in the branch that

00:35:08.761 --> 00:35:13.080
helps us to design
a system that works.

00:35:13.081 --> 00:35:16.095
We have multiple feedback
mechanisms as well.

00:35:16.096 --> 00:35:18.230
We have different ways

00:35:18.231 --> 00:35:20.685
that the developers can
have their voice be heard.

00:35:20.686 --> 00:35:23.150
We regularly pull them
and find out what is

00:35:23.151 --> 00:35:24.880
the most important thing to

00:35:24.881 --> 00:35:27.275
deal with next,
and we chase that.

00:35:27.276 --> 00:35:29.900
Oddly enough,
the most important thing

00:35:29.901 --> 00:35:32.330
to chase right now is
documentation for us.

00:35:32.331 --> 00:35:34.815
Not even an engineering problem,

00:35:34.816 --> 00:35:36.880
documentation is
what the developers

00:35:36.881 --> 00:35:38.225
are least pleased with now.

00:35:38.226 --> 00:35:40.230
So we're making
investments in that to

00:35:40.231 --> 00:35:41.280
improve it and there's multiple

00:35:41.281 --> 00:35:43.550
different ways that
we're trying to do it.

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:47.460
The end. Thank you.

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:57.460
&gt;&gt; Yes, so it's
no coincidence that everyone,

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:00.640
no coordination between
Chap and Laurie but

00:36:00.641 --> 00:36:02.150
the problems are exactly

00:36:02.151 --> 00:36:03.580
the same across most
of the industry.

00:36:03.581 --> 00:36:06.690
I think it's quite funny you
talked about documentation.

00:36:06.691 --> 00:36:12.045
Irene did a study and
somebody talked about,

00:36:12.046 --> 00:36:16.430
"Do software and engineering
is very similar to cooking?

00:36:16.431 --> 00:36:18.850
And writing the software
is basically

00:36:18.851 --> 00:36:20.200
the actual cooking itself and

00:36:20.201 --> 00:36:21.650
documentation is the washing up?"

00:36:21.651 --> 00:36:24.045
So, that's basically.

00:36:24.046 --> 00:36:26.100
Finally, we have Office

00:36:26.101 --> 00:36:29.190
on the many different
problems with Office.

00:36:29.191 --> 00:36:29.745
All right, Jen.

00:36:29.746 --> 00:36:32.790
&gt;&gt; Right. I'm
Jennifer Beckmann and

00:36:32.791 --> 00:36:36.345
I am Office Engineering Manager.

00:36:36.346 --> 00:36:37.950
I actually previously worked in

00:36:37.951 --> 00:36:40.430
Bing with Chap and we drove

00:36:40.431 --> 00:36:42.660
that experimentation number
up to thousands of

00:36:42.661 --> 00:36:45.370
experiments so it was
lots of fun over there.

00:36:45.371 --> 00:36:47.190
Now, I've moved to Office,

00:36:47.191 --> 00:36:48.945
I'm in the Office
Fundamentals Group,

00:36:48.946 --> 00:36:52.780
which is responsible for
the build, the flighting,

00:36:52.781 --> 00:36:56.565
and experimentation,
telemetry, security,

00:36:56.566 --> 00:37:01.840
and performance pieces of Office.

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:05.650
Seeing this talk that
we are very far from

00:37:05.651 --> 00:37:10.820
being the nice agility that
they've driven over there,

00:37:10.821 --> 00:37:13.130
and that's what I'm
excited about doing as

00:37:13.131 --> 00:37:17.080
I most recently moved
to Office in December.

00:37:17.081 --> 00:37:19.995
But I've been working
with Office for a while.

00:37:19.996 --> 00:37:22.295
So when I talk about Office,

00:37:22.296 --> 00:37:24.510
you might be wondering, well,

00:37:24.511 --> 00:37:26.055
what do you talk about
in terms of Office,

00:37:26.056 --> 00:37:28.840
SharePoint or is it the clients?

00:37:28.841 --> 00:37:30.230
I'm talking about
the rich clients

00:37:30.231 --> 00:37:32.170
so Word, Excel, and PowerPoint.

00:37:32.171 --> 00:37:33.950
That's the group that I work

00:37:33.951 --> 00:37:36.355
in and that's what I'll
be talking about today.

00:37:36.356 --> 00:37:38.560
Exchange and the other services

00:37:38.561 --> 00:37:39.850
are whole different story,

00:37:39.851 --> 00:37:42.920
they have a different story
around continuous deployment.

00:37:42.921 --> 00:37:45.355
But I'm really talking
about the apps,

00:37:45.356 --> 00:37:47.700
the apps that are on
your desktop right now,

00:37:47.701 --> 00:37:49.600
what you most recently made

00:37:49.601 --> 00:37:51.385
your PowerPoint presentation in.

00:37:51.386 --> 00:37:52.670
That is what I'm talking about.

00:37:52.671 --> 00:37:54.515
How are we dealing with

00:37:54.516 --> 00:37:57.520
continuous deployment
and delivery in Office.

00:37:57.521 --> 00:38:03.740
I'm going to rewind us
and put us back to 1990.

00:38:03.741 --> 00:38:05.895
That was when
the first Office Suite

00:38:05.896 --> 00:38:08.110
shipped together, Word,
Excel, PowerPoint.

00:38:08.111 --> 00:38:09.910
Before that, two releases

00:38:09.911 --> 00:38:11.675
before that shipped
independently,

00:38:11.676 --> 00:38:14.310
and in 1990, they put them
all together in a suite and

00:38:14.311 --> 00:38:17.055
sold them together as Office.

00:38:17.056 --> 00:38:21.520
That was 28 years ago and
we have shipped Office,

00:38:21.521 --> 00:38:24.030
her versions of Office every
two to three years since

00:38:24.031 --> 00:38:27.740
then for around 11
different releases.

00:38:27.860 --> 00:38:32.310
Its about one release
every three years,

00:38:32.311 --> 00:38:37.585
the ship cycle was the product
managers who get together,

00:38:37.586 --> 00:38:40.185
figure out what features
should go in the product,

00:38:40.186 --> 00:38:43.070
developers develop it, and then

00:38:43.071 --> 00:38:46.215
we would release it
internally to dog food.

00:38:46.216 --> 00:38:50.040
Get feedback, bug fixes,
and then externally,

00:38:50.041 --> 00:38:53.050
to audience called
MVPs or insiders,

00:38:53.051 --> 00:38:54.150
which are outside of

00:38:54.151 --> 00:38:57.820
Microsoft but they are
close collaborators,

00:38:57.821 --> 00:38:59.135
they would give us feedback.

00:38:59.136 --> 00:39:00.860
We would fix it, stabilize,

00:39:00.861 --> 00:39:02.250
and then release it in

00:39:02.251 --> 00:39:05.870
a process which we called
RTM release to media.

00:39:05.871 --> 00:39:07.700
CDs, discs.

00:39:07.701 --> 00:39:10.525
So, that's how most of

00:39:10.526 --> 00:39:15.130
Microsoft ship products
over this time frame.

00:39:15.131 --> 00:39:17.640
The first version of Office in

00:39:17.641 --> 00:39:20.940
1990 shipped in
both Windows and Mac.

00:39:20.941 --> 00:39:24.570
Mac and Windows were
two different product units,

00:39:24.571 --> 00:39:26.175
two different sets of people,

00:39:26.176 --> 00:39:28.450
two different codebases shipping

00:39:28.451 --> 00:39:32.275
independently throughout
most of the time.

00:39:32.276 --> 00:39:34.520
The web version of
Windows are when

00:39:34.521 --> 00:39:37.510
we have Office shipped in 2008,

00:39:37.511 --> 00:39:41.555
and the iPhone and Android
version shipped in 2013,

00:39:41.556 --> 00:39:44.600
and we handle it all in
the same way at the time.

00:39:44.601 --> 00:39:46.370
Office is the mainline
Windows branch,

00:39:46.371 --> 00:39:47.520
we'd fork the code and

00:39:47.521 --> 00:39:48.780
then they would
work really hard to

00:39:48.781 --> 00:39:51.670
make it work on the platform
that was targeted.

00:39:51.671 --> 00:39:53.790
That's where our office
is coming from,

00:39:53.791 --> 00:39:56.770
that's where most of
the code base was built at C++.

00:39:56.771 --> 00:39:58.135
It's a huge code base

00:39:58.136 --> 00:39:59.750
and I'll give you
some numbers later.

00:39:59.751 --> 00:40:04.950
But in 2015, I pulled up

00:40:04.951 --> 00:40:07.570
the Microsoft earning report and

00:40:07.571 --> 00:40:10.800
it gives an indication of
where Office was at that time.

00:40:10.801 --> 00:40:15.025
Windows XP had just
expired its support,

00:40:15.026 --> 00:40:18.500
there was major declines
in the PC business market.

00:40:18.501 --> 00:40:21.850
Windows and Office were
often sold together,

00:40:21.851 --> 00:40:24.500
so if Windows declined
Office declined,

00:40:24.501 --> 00:40:27.230
and that's what
happened to Office.

00:40:27.231 --> 00:40:31.360
Windows declined, we saw
the decline in earnings from

00:40:31.361 --> 00:40:34.440
Office and also our customers

00:40:34.441 --> 00:40:36.590
we're transitioning to O 365,

00:40:36.591 --> 00:40:38.825
which is our
subscription service.

00:40:38.826 --> 00:40:46.190
In October 2015, that's when
we shipped Office 2016.

00:40:46.191 --> 00:40:47.750
This is when we already

00:40:47.751 --> 00:40:50.150
knew these things were
happening and this is where

00:40:50.151 --> 00:40:51.810
we started to integrate some

00:40:51.811 --> 00:40:53.450
of that continuous deployment

00:40:53.451 --> 00:40:57.110
and delivery practices that
we were just heard about.

00:40:57.111 --> 00:40:58.940
So not too long ago.

00:40:58.941 --> 00:41:03.120
So let me give you an idea
of what Office has

00:41:03.121 --> 00:41:08.155
been doing and working
on with Office 2016.

00:41:08.156 --> 00:41:10.790
I mentioned before
that each one of

00:41:10.791 --> 00:41:14.380
the products for shipping
have a different codebase.

00:41:14.381 --> 00:41:16.370
Each independently forked,

00:41:16.371 --> 00:41:18.305
ran by different teams.

00:41:18.306 --> 00:41:23.520
In Office 2016, ever
since we shipped in 2015,

00:41:23.521 --> 00:41:25.020
we've been working
on trying to get

00:41:25.021 --> 00:41:27.580
all the code into one codebase.

00:41:27.581 --> 00:41:30.235
This is not entirely true
but we're working on it.

00:41:30.236 --> 00:41:32.035
Most recently,

00:41:32.036 --> 00:41:36.230
Mac was integrated into
the Windows codebase.

00:41:36.231 --> 00:41:40.570
So, Mac, Windows both
shipped from the same code.

00:41:40.571 --> 00:41:42.805
That was January of this year,

00:41:42.806 --> 00:41:44.370
first time that Mac

00:41:44.371 --> 00:41:48.570
shipped with Windows
in January this year.

00:41:48.571 --> 00:41:50.360
So, it's a really
huge accomplishment.

00:41:50.361 --> 00:41:52.070
The team has been
working on it for

00:41:52.071 --> 00:41:56.425
four years getting the code
together in one place.

00:41:56.426 --> 00:41:58.885
In terms of shipping the product,

00:41:58.886 --> 00:42:00.335
like I talked about before,

00:42:00.336 --> 00:42:02.350
you would bundle all
the features together and say,

00:42:02.351 --> 00:42:04.435
"Surprise, these are
all your features."

00:42:04.436 --> 00:42:08.820
Now, we're shipping Office

00:42:08.821 --> 00:42:12.015
12 times a year instead of
once every three years,

00:42:12.016 --> 00:42:14.975
and that's approximately
every month.

00:42:14.976 --> 00:42:17.860
The features are
behind the flags.

00:42:17.861 --> 00:42:19.370
They're dark. They're not turned

00:42:19.371 --> 00:42:22.240
on for our users
until they're ready.

00:42:22.241 --> 00:42:26.330
They're out there exposed
with percentage-wise,

00:42:26.331 --> 00:42:28.630
so that we can watch to make
sure the quality is high

00:42:28.631 --> 00:42:32.550
enough before they
get to everybody.

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:36.235
By the numbers, we are

00:42:36.236 --> 00:42:38.500
slightly bigger than [inaudible]
looking at their nose.

00:42:38.501 --> 00:42:41.430
Wow, we're quite big.

00:42:41.431 --> 00:42:43.740
So, we have 4,000 developers.

00:42:43.741 --> 00:42:46.010
We check in one checkin

00:42:46.011 --> 00:42:48.860
or I check in
every 20 seconds or so.

00:42:48.861 --> 00:42:52.915
We have 1,000 checkins a day.

00:42:52.916 --> 00:42:56.525
The codebase is
half a billion lines of code.

00:42:56.526 --> 00:43:00.390
Not just code, but
test automation suites,

00:43:00.391 --> 00:43:02.660
language proofing is huge.

00:43:02.661 --> 00:43:05.410
Tools and engineering systems is

00:43:05.411 --> 00:43:08.460
also contributing a lot
to that number there.

00:43:08.461 --> 00:43:12.155
The Test Suite runs
about 20,000 jobs,

00:43:12.156 --> 00:43:14.520
tens of thousands of scenarios,

00:43:14.521 --> 00:43:17.135
and hundreds of thousands
of different configurations

00:43:17.136 --> 00:43:20.930
every day to get it
out to customers.

00:43:20.931 --> 00:43:24.590
Oh, my field slides
aren't working.

00:43:24.930 --> 00:43:27.070
It's probably a reflection

00:43:27.071 --> 00:43:30.740
of a feature being exposed wrong.

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:37.315
So, how do we get features
out to customers?

00:43:37.316 --> 00:43:38.750
It's actually not a lot different

00:43:38.751 --> 00:43:40.110
than what I described before.

00:43:40.111 --> 00:43:41.520
We still have audiences.

00:43:41.521 --> 00:43:45.540
Dogfood is internal set of
users within Microsoft.

00:43:45.541 --> 00:43:46.780
Microsoft gets the product,

00:43:46.781 --> 00:43:49.740
then it goes out to insiders

00:43:49.741 --> 00:43:53.665
similar to our MBP audience
that we had before,

00:43:53.666 --> 00:43:55.095
and then it goes
out to production.

00:43:55.096 --> 00:43:58.770
So, those mechanics
are a relatively sane.

00:43:58.771 --> 00:44:02.000
What's different is that we're
trying to ship every day.

00:44:02.001 --> 00:44:06.050
We're trying to ship a
mainline-built every day.

00:44:06.051 --> 00:44:08.975
Out to production, it's monthly.

00:44:08.976 --> 00:44:11.870
This cadence and
velocity is a lot

00:44:11.871 --> 00:44:14.980
faster than what we
were able to do before.

00:44:14.981 --> 00:44:17.940
Also, we have feature
toggles which

00:44:17.941 --> 00:44:20.100
is brand new into Office 2016.

00:44:20.101 --> 00:44:22.430
So, having Dart code and

00:44:22.431 --> 00:44:26.970
then rolling it out and giving it

00:44:26.971 --> 00:44:30.380
to our customers within the
build is brand new and it's

00:44:30.381 --> 00:44:34.625
something that we've been
doing a lot of in Office 2016.

00:44:34.626 --> 00:44:37.005
Just to make things
more complicated,

00:44:37.006 --> 00:44:40.880
we also split our user-based
into different channels.

00:44:40.881 --> 00:44:43.475
So, we have our monthly channel.

00:44:43.476 --> 00:44:44.950
It's mainly consumers.

00:44:44.951 --> 00:44:49.035
These people are
your mom and dad and

00:44:49.036 --> 00:44:50.765
yourself maybe who've bought

00:44:50.766 --> 00:44:53.540
your PC at Costco and
have Office on it.

00:44:53.541 --> 00:44:55.420
You'll get updates every month

00:44:55.421 --> 00:44:58.905
through the monthly
consumer channel.

00:44:58.906 --> 00:45:01.890
The inner prizes are
a lot more risk averse.

00:45:01.891 --> 00:45:05.040
So, they don't want to take
the cutting edge stuff.

00:45:05.041 --> 00:45:06.350
They've never wanted to do that.

00:45:06.351 --> 00:45:10.230
So, they take a build
every six months internally,

00:45:10.231 --> 00:45:13.030
test it and then they deploy

00:45:13.031 --> 00:45:14.865
that six month build within

00:45:14.866 --> 00:45:18.390
the organizations for
stability reasons.

00:45:18.391 --> 00:45:20.550
We are doing a lot
of investments in

00:45:20.551 --> 00:45:22.370
enterprise in this space.

00:45:22.371 --> 00:45:24.060
I won't talk about
it here too much,

00:45:24.061 --> 00:45:26.110
but giving the visibility to

00:45:26.111 --> 00:45:28.610
the enterprises of how
well the build is doing

00:45:28.611 --> 00:45:31.100
within the organization
is something we've been

00:45:31.101 --> 00:45:34.625
doing with Microsoft 365.

00:45:34.626 --> 00:45:38.270
It's called, it's Microsoft 365.

00:45:38.271 --> 00:45:40.180
If you look it up on the web,

00:45:40.181 --> 00:45:45.190
it's interesting twist on
continuous deployment.

00:45:45.191 --> 00:45:49.380
All right. So, there's a lot of

00:45:49.381 --> 00:45:51.800
challenges with doing
continuous deployment

00:45:51.801 --> 00:45:53.890
like I just described
with Office.

00:45:53.891 --> 00:45:56.995
The codebase itself
is written in C++,

00:45:56.996 --> 00:46:00.265
and a lot of it is 28-years-old.

00:46:00.266 --> 00:46:03.870
So, the developers that are
working on Office right now,

00:46:03.871 --> 00:46:07.020
a lot of them are younger
than the code itself.

00:46:07.021 --> 00:46:08.995
So, things that we

00:46:08.996 --> 00:46:11.120
take for granted or
Bing takes for granted,

00:46:11.121 --> 00:46:14.255
for example, are just
not there in the code.

00:46:14.256 --> 00:46:16.620
Telemetry, being one of them.

00:46:16.621 --> 00:46:17.950
Oftentimes, when things break,

00:46:17.951 --> 00:46:20.030
we're not sure how bad
it's broken because we

00:46:20.031 --> 00:46:22.895
just don't have
the data to tell us.

00:46:22.896 --> 00:46:26.125
We don't have a fast build
and release process.

00:46:26.126 --> 00:46:27.740
Our build takes about five hours

00:46:27.741 --> 00:46:29.450
and they worked really
hard to bring that down.

00:46:29.451 --> 00:46:31.685
They did a great job doing that.

00:46:31.686 --> 00:46:35.284
The ticket assigned
build that's tested,

00:46:35.285 --> 00:46:38.545
it takes four days

00:46:38.546 --> 00:46:41.480
and then getting that
filled out saturated

00:46:41.481 --> 00:46:44.010
to all of our
protection audiences is

00:46:44.011 --> 00:46:48.745
about 30 days on
the monthly channels.

00:46:48.746 --> 00:46:51.470
Test coverage is another issue

00:46:51.471 --> 00:46:53.475
with a such an old codebase.

00:46:53.476 --> 00:46:57.070
The previous incarnations of

00:46:57.071 --> 00:47:00.410
our organizations had
test organization.

00:47:00.411 --> 00:47:03.360
Oftentimes,
manual tests were ran.

00:47:03.361 --> 00:47:05.360
Person sitting in their Office

00:47:05.361 --> 00:47:06.510
would click through the product,

00:47:06.511 --> 00:47:08.300
it was never codified.

00:47:08.301 --> 00:47:12.530
So, some of the tests
are just missing.

00:47:12.531 --> 00:47:16.990
So, we don't have as much
confidence in the builds,

00:47:16.991 --> 00:47:18.840
sometimes, if changes were made

00:47:18.841 --> 00:47:21.730
in places that don't
have that coverage.

00:47:21.731 --> 00:47:24.755
The DevOps pieces of

00:47:24.756 --> 00:47:27.090
continuous deployment
that were talked about is

00:47:27.091 --> 00:47:28.520
also challenging in Office

00:47:28.521 --> 00:47:32.210
because as we build and
deploy these releases,

00:47:32.211 --> 00:47:33.980
they are not in our data centers.

00:47:33.981 --> 00:47:37.430
So, it's not a situation
where we have a previous,

00:47:37.431 --> 00:47:40.100
current, and next
build that's deployed.

00:47:40.101 --> 00:47:42.680
We have, the hundreds
of builds that we

00:47:42.681 --> 00:47:44.630
produced and maintained in

00:47:44.631 --> 00:47:46.770
all of the different
platforms out there.

00:47:46.771 --> 00:47:50.030
Sometimes, with
shared code across

00:47:50.031 --> 00:47:54.825
web, mobile, and Windows.

00:47:54.826 --> 00:47:58.370
So, as an engineer who's
responsible for DevOps,

00:47:58.371 --> 00:48:00.010
it's really hard to be able to

00:48:00.011 --> 00:48:02.935
tell what version
of the code was it,

00:48:02.936 --> 00:48:05.470
and what could be
really going wrong.

00:48:05.471 --> 00:48:07.170
So, it's really challenging.

00:48:07.171 --> 00:48:11.425
A lot of the differences

00:48:11.426 --> 00:48:13.990
and challenges that
we have in moving

00:48:13.991 --> 00:48:17.220
to continuous deployment
and integration has to

00:48:17.221 --> 00:48:21.455
do with just historical
organizational culture.

00:48:21.456 --> 00:48:24.690
The technologies that were

00:48:24.691 --> 00:48:27.429
used in each one of
these different platforms,

00:48:27.430 --> 00:48:31.150
so web and windows have
different telemetry stacks.

00:48:31.151 --> 00:48:33.120
They have a different way of

00:48:33.121 --> 00:48:35.830
ingesting and spitting
out telemetry.

00:48:35.831 --> 00:48:39.950
So, you can't necessarily
compare the two of them.

00:48:39.951 --> 00:48:44.485
So, we worked through trying
to standardize telemetry,

00:48:44.486 --> 00:48:46.500
trying to standardize
the different metrics.

00:48:46.501 --> 00:48:47.770
So, we'll have often

00:48:47.771 --> 00:48:51.660
conversations about how
to get to one thing.

00:48:51.661 --> 00:48:54.755
The different platforms also have

00:48:54.756 --> 00:48:56.640
different philosophies
about what it

00:48:56.641 --> 00:48:59.275
means to close up
build-blocking bug,

00:48:59.276 --> 00:49:02.665
what it means to run unit tests.

00:49:02.666 --> 00:49:07.695
The Mac team has a great culture
around unit tests.

00:49:07.696 --> 00:49:11.320
They force every build to go run

00:49:11.321 --> 00:49:13.265
all the unit tests and will

00:49:13.266 --> 00:49:17.375
fail deployment if
anyone unit tests fails.

00:49:17.376 --> 00:49:20.715
Windows is so big

00:49:20.716 --> 00:49:22.400
and the unit tests are so

00:49:22.401 --> 00:49:25.495
flaky that they don't even
want their unit tests to run.

00:49:25.496 --> 00:49:30.935
So, the two different cultures
as they grew up over time,

00:49:30.936 --> 00:49:35.180
just grew up with different ways
of handling situations.

00:49:35.181 --> 00:49:38.100
We will run into
this almost daily,

00:49:38.101 --> 00:49:40.020
but just people in a room having

00:49:40.021 --> 00:49:41.540
different philosophies and having

00:49:41.541 --> 00:49:43.800
to work through those issues.

00:49:44.130 --> 00:49:46.380
Just to wrap up,

00:49:46.381 --> 00:49:48.570
we have a lot of
market pressure to

00:49:48.571 --> 00:49:52.050
continually develop
new functionality within Office.

00:49:52.051 --> 00:49:54.820
Some of the cool features
around collaboration are

00:49:54.821 --> 00:50:00.940
coming if you don't already
have them right now.

00:50:00.941 --> 00:50:04.030
It's really fun to be in Office

00:50:04.031 --> 00:50:06.410
and be able to deliver
these things in

00:50:06.411 --> 00:50:08.640
this new world where
we're trying to ship

00:50:08.641 --> 00:50:12.930
faster builds of the product.

00:50:12.931 --> 00:50:16.510
Within there, we're also

00:50:16.511 --> 00:50:17.780
investing in different ways

00:50:17.781 --> 00:50:20.270
of developing Office experiences.

00:50:20.271 --> 00:50:22.570
Laurie was talking about

00:50:22.571 --> 00:50:25.410
other products also
having this challenge.

00:50:25.411 --> 00:50:27.880
We have this really
old codebase, C++.

00:50:27.881 --> 00:50:31.010
It's really expensive to
develop a new feature in

00:50:31.011 --> 00:50:34.530
the core C++ environment

00:50:34.531 --> 00:50:36.600
and then deploy it and get
it out to our customers.

00:50:36.601 --> 00:50:38.300
So, we're looking
at different ways

00:50:38.301 --> 00:50:40.740
of developing within Office.

00:50:40.741 --> 00:50:46.195
Add-ins is a way that we're
also tackling this problem.

00:50:46.196 --> 00:50:51.230
Add-ins are a third-party way
of adding to Office.

00:50:51.231 --> 00:50:54.460
So, third parties
even in Office 2015,

00:50:54.461 --> 00:50:57.515
they were able to add
new functionality to Office.

00:50:57.516 --> 00:50:59.960
But it wasn't
the first class with

00:50:59.961 --> 00:51:03.780
the Office developed
and deployed features,

00:51:03.781 --> 00:51:07.830
but we're looking at
it as an avenue to

00:51:07.831 --> 00:51:11.690
getting features out there
more quickly as a first party,

00:51:11.691 --> 00:51:13.470
or Microsoft developers,

00:51:13.471 --> 00:51:16.635
experience of adding to
Office through Add-ins.

00:51:16.636 --> 00:51:21.180
Add-ins are used, it's
called the Agave framework.

00:51:21.181 --> 00:51:22.310
If you search on the web,

00:51:22.311 --> 00:51:25.875
you'll be able to find it
and it's JavaScript-based.

00:51:25.876 --> 00:51:28.660
So, small packages
are delivered to

00:51:28.661 --> 00:51:31.654
the client who run
JavaScript locally.

00:51:31.655 --> 00:51:34.870
There's a huge effort
there to have

00:51:34.871 --> 00:51:38.165
that as our next way of
adding features to Office,

00:51:38.166 --> 00:51:41.000
and then we're doing
more service-based experiences

00:51:41.001 --> 00:51:42.990
delivered to the clients.

00:51:42.991 --> 00:51:45.460
The more obvious one
is the "Help".

00:51:45.461 --> 00:51:46.790
At the very top, there's

00:51:46.791 --> 00:51:51.250
a little light bulb

00:51:51.251 --> 00:51:52.400
and you can ask question of

00:51:52.401 --> 00:51:55.300
the service space
integration with client.

00:51:55.301 --> 00:51:57.075
We have integrations with Bing.

00:51:57.076 --> 00:51:59.230
When we do a service-based
integrations,

00:51:59.231 --> 00:52:00.655
it's much easier
because we're able to

00:52:00.656 --> 00:52:02.690
deploy a lot faster
and go through

00:52:02.691 --> 00:52:06.090
the services delivery
pipelines instead

00:52:06.091 --> 00:52:10.490
of dealing with
the big fat clients.

00:52:10.491 --> 00:52:14.670
So, we're having a lot
of fun making Office,

00:52:14.671 --> 00:52:16.280
optimizing what we do have,

00:52:16.281 --> 00:52:19.550
finding new ways of getting
features out to customers.

00:52:19.551 --> 00:52:22.425
Those are some of
our continuous deployment

00:52:22.426 --> 00:52:23.660
in delivery stories.

00:52:23.661 --> 00:52:29.485
Thanks.

00:52:29.486 --> 00:52:34.960
&gt;&gt;So, we now do questions.

00:52:34.961 --> 00:52:38.120
You can question the speakers.

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:41.365
&gt;&gt; Okay, does that work?

00:52:41.366 --> 00:52:45.530
&gt;&gt; That's fine. Yes.

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:50.450
Say your name.

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:55.105
&gt;&gt; Great talks. Do I
have to push anything?

00:52:55.106 --> 00:52:57.140
There we go. Great talks.

00:52:57.141 --> 00:52:58.650
One of the questions I had,

00:52:58.651 --> 00:52:59.780
you guys have heard of

00:52:59.781 --> 00:53:02.070
the night group disaster
with feature toggles,

00:53:02.071 --> 00:53:04.210
where they had
an old toggle turned on and

00:53:04.211 --> 00:53:07.120
lost $460 million in 45 minutes.

00:53:07.121 --> 00:53:08.930
So, how do you deal
with when you're

00:53:08.931 --> 00:53:10.630
doing thousands of experiments,

00:53:10.631 --> 00:53:13.210
how do you make sure
that this feature

00:53:13.211 --> 00:53:15.870
is running on this person's
machine, on this server,

00:53:15.871 --> 00:53:17.970
and this feature is running
on that server and they're

00:53:17.971 --> 00:53:21.675
not sending any corrupted data
or doing something bad?

00:53:21.676 --> 00:53:23.050
How do you deal with

00:53:23.051 --> 00:53:26.120
the different combinations
of feature toggles?

00:53:27.270 --> 00:53:31.750
&gt;&gt; My question. Yes.
So, my team owns

00:53:31.751 --> 00:53:34.380
the feature toggle API for Office

00:53:34.381 --> 00:53:36.790
and I have to say
it's really hard.

00:53:36.791 --> 00:53:39.455
There is a ton of complexity

00:53:39.456 --> 00:53:42.830
around what is being
tested in animation and

00:53:42.831 --> 00:53:45.380
who has what and dog foods

00:53:45.381 --> 00:53:49.200
who is a problem that
we do deal with.

00:53:49.201 --> 00:53:50.885
My experience with Bing,

00:53:50.886 --> 00:53:54.230
when I was in Bing
was that especially,

00:53:54.231 --> 00:53:56.910
with experiments
having a control and

00:53:56.911 --> 00:53:59.840
a treatment even if you
plan to ship the feature,

00:53:59.841 --> 00:54:01.375
you're much more confident

00:54:01.376 --> 00:54:04.280
in the fact that
it is the feature

00:54:04.281 --> 00:54:06.200
that's causing
the problem and then you

00:54:06.201 --> 00:54:09.070
focus on making the data faster.

00:54:09.071 --> 00:54:11.110
So, all the signals
around crashes,

00:54:11.111 --> 00:54:14.430
especially in Bing we
worked really hard

00:54:14.431 --> 00:54:17.870
to get it down to minutes for

00:54:17.871 --> 00:54:21.245
those really strong signals
of something going wrong

00:54:21.246 --> 00:54:23.460
and ideally in Office we'll get

00:54:23.461 --> 00:54:25.860
there. Do you have
something to add?

00:54:25.861 --> 00:54:27.930
&gt;&gt; Yes, I think
Laurie's presentation

00:54:27.931 --> 00:54:29.375
talked a little bit about it.

00:54:29.376 --> 00:54:31.120
&gt;&gt; Yes, but it is

00:54:31.121 --> 00:54:33.910
complex and you have
those guard rail metrics.

00:54:33.911 --> 00:54:35.730
Basically, something's
wrong, shut it

00:54:35.731 --> 00:54:39.170
off to try to prevent
something like that.

00:54:39.171 --> 00:54:41.730
But I do think that we
need to acknowledge

00:54:41.731 --> 00:54:43.200
the level of complexity

00:54:43.201 --> 00:54:45.020
that is being put
into the code bases,

00:54:45.021 --> 00:54:46.290
which is not something that

00:54:46.291 --> 00:54:48.555
we traditionally have dealt with.

00:54:48.556 --> 00:54:52.625
There are companies like
Facebook at the summit said

00:54:52.626 --> 00:54:55.060
they they plan that

00:54:55.061 --> 00:54:57.300
those feature toggles
will stay there forever,

00:54:57.301 --> 00:54:59.150
they don't ever plan
to take them away.

00:54:59.151 --> 00:55:02.470
So, the impact of that type
of development and

00:55:02.471 --> 00:55:05.820
all of the company who
prevalently use feature toggle,

00:55:05.821 --> 00:55:10.200
which is a lot, it's
a different way to develop.

00:55:10.201 --> 00:55:14.470
We should be teaching
differently because of that.

00:55:14.560 --> 00:55:17.580
&gt;&gt; We recognize at Bing that one

00:55:17.581 --> 00:55:19.600
it's not even
just an experiment by

00:55:19.601 --> 00:55:21.780
itself but one experiment can

00:55:21.781 --> 00:55:24.195
have a side effect on
another existing experiment.

00:55:24.196 --> 00:55:26.065
So, one of the final gates

00:55:26.066 --> 00:55:29.045
to turning on an experiment
in production,

00:55:29.046 --> 00:55:30.750
is we will rerun all of

00:55:30.751 --> 00:55:33.960
the existing tests and
force that experiment,

00:55:33.961 --> 00:55:36.050
in addition to
whatever experiments to

00:55:36.051 --> 00:55:38.755
the test we're actually testing
so you get that coverage.

00:55:38.756 --> 00:55:40.910
They'll know ahead of
time if any tests fail.

00:55:40.911 --> 00:55:42.055
We expect them all to pass,

00:55:42.056 --> 00:55:44.585
so they'll know ahead of
time if any tests fail.

00:55:44.586 --> 00:55:46.550
They can look at
their results and decide

00:55:46.551 --> 00:55:48.760
whether it's a transient issue,

00:55:48.761 --> 00:55:50.665
or it's a real problem.

00:55:50.666 --> 00:55:54.920
We also do the polite shutdown.

00:55:54.921 --> 00:55:57.150
So there's a handful of business

00:55:57.151 --> 00:55:59.350
specific critical metrics like

00:55:59.351 --> 00:56:01.530
revenue that we will know within

00:56:01.531 --> 00:56:03.330
a few minutes based on

00:56:03.331 --> 00:56:05.895
the control and the treatment
and it just gets shut down.

00:56:05.896 --> 00:56:09.000
It's an instantaneous shut down.

00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:17.430
&gt;&gt; One of the
fundamental problems

00:56:17.431 --> 00:56:18.560
in product development is

00:56:18.561 --> 00:56:22.090
that a lot of the functionality
is almost never used.

00:56:22.091 --> 00:56:25.950
So, that's not only
Microsoft products

00:56:25.951 --> 00:56:28.510
but one of the problems.

00:56:28.750 --> 00:56:31.640
We have looked into research to

00:56:31.641 --> 00:56:35.245
study which functionality
could be taken out.

00:56:35.246 --> 00:56:38.330
I wonder how the
continuous deployment,

00:56:38.331 --> 00:56:42.370
continuous integration
paradigm accommodates

00:56:42.371 --> 00:56:46.420
this idea. Is there any?

00:56:46.421 --> 00:56:48.560
&gt;&gt; Dead code is
a problem if we're

00:56:48.561 --> 00:56:50.925
doing it thousands of
experiments per month.

00:56:50.926 --> 00:56:52.380
&gt;&gt; And that includes elimination.

00:56:52.381 --> 00:56:53.925
&gt;&gt; It is dead code. I mean,

00:56:53.926 --> 00:56:56.640
we would say comfortably
maybe 10 percent or

00:56:56.641 --> 00:56:59.590
less of those experiments
as successful but we try.

00:56:59.591 --> 00:57:02.190
Doesn't mean that when
the flight gets turned off,

00:57:02.191 --> 00:57:03.140
or the experiment finishes

00:57:03.141 --> 00:57:04.270
that they go and
clean up the code.

00:57:04.271 --> 00:57:06.655
In fact, I've been doing work

00:57:06.656 --> 00:57:10.630
myself to reclaim some of that,

00:57:10.631 --> 00:57:13.355
some of the dead config and to

00:57:13.356 --> 00:57:14.640
work with the partner
teams to clean

00:57:14.641 --> 00:57:16.170
up their related code.

00:57:16.171 --> 00:57:17.755
It's a problem, it's
a real problem.

00:57:17.756 --> 00:57:18.880
It's a long-term problem that

00:57:18.881 --> 00:57:20.920
just adds to our working set,

00:57:20.921 --> 00:57:23.390
it slows our requests
down slightly,

00:57:23.391 --> 00:57:25.660
and death of a thousand cuts.

00:57:25.661 --> 00:57:27.000
Now we're 10 milliseconds slower,

00:57:27.001 --> 00:57:29.210
now we're 20 milliseconds slower.

00:57:29.211 --> 00:57:32.660
&gt;&gt; Yes, I think it's
an exciting development to have

00:57:32.661 --> 00:57:37.715
things updated 100 times
in 100 milliseconds.

00:57:37.716 --> 00:57:40.290
On the other hand, is it really

00:57:40.291 --> 00:57:42.150
the the direction to

00:57:42.151 --> 00:57:44.690
go to make it faster,
faster, faster, faster?

00:57:44.691 --> 00:57:47.340
I mean, to me it
looks sometimes it is

00:57:47.341 --> 00:57:51.290
very incremental changes and
we need on the other hand,

00:57:51.291 --> 00:57:53.020
certainly, a more global

00:57:53.021 --> 00:57:55.545
perspective on
the product, right?

00:57:55.546 --> 00:57:59.005
We're all in it too.

00:57:59.006 --> 00:58:01.470
&gt;&gt; It will interesting in
the long run what will happen

00:58:01.471 --> 00:58:04.030
for that functionality
that was never used.

00:58:04.031 --> 00:58:06.640
I've seen Standish
Group statistics

00:58:06.641 --> 00:58:09.025
on that same thing and it's,

00:58:09.026 --> 00:58:11.085
60 percent of the code,

00:58:11.086 --> 00:58:13.080
the features are never used.

00:58:13.081 --> 00:58:16.310
So hopefully, with
the experimentation then that is

00:58:16.311 --> 00:58:18.535
discovered and the mechanism

00:58:18.536 --> 00:58:20.600
are in place to just shut it off,

00:58:20.601 --> 00:58:23.100
where before that
functionality was embedded in

00:58:23.101 --> 00:58:26.650
the product and so you could
easily just shut it off.

00:58:26.651 --> 00:58:29.420
So hopefully, that will be
the case and as mentioned,

00:58:29.421 --> 00:58:30.620
I guess you said something like

00:58:30.621 --> 00:58:31.920
10 percent of the experiments

00:58:31.921 --> 00:58:35.170
are successes and Netflix
will say the same thing.

00:58:35.171 --> 00:58:38.265
I think that you
had a study in a,

00:58:38.266 --> 00:58:41.095
was it Office that
was 30 percent work?

00:58:41.096 --> 00:58:41.850
&gt;&gt; It's Bing.

00:58:41.851 --> 00:58:42.510
&gt;&gt; It's Bing.

00:58:42.511 --> 00:58:46.115
&gt;&gt; Bing, okay. So, there's
an acknowledgement that

00:58:46.116 --> 00:58:47.830
a large percentage of
the features that are

00:58:47.831 --> 00:58:51.450
developed are not deemed
to be an improvement.

00:58:51.451 --> 00:58:53.770
So then, they are shut off which

00:58:53.771 --> 00:58:56.450
would prevent the features
that are never used.

00:58:56.451 --> 00:58:58.660
It doesn't prevent
the technical death.

00:58:58.661 --> 00:59:00.430
&gt;&gt; Yes. But you may

00:59:00.431 --> 00:59:02.000
have been asking
a slightly different question.

00:59:02.001 --> 00:59:03.640
Are you saying that it's not

00:59:03.641 --> 00:59:05.690
always best to chase latency,

00:59:05.691 --> 00:59:07.340
that you may have
to sacrifice some

00:59:07.341 --> 00:59:09.555
of it for feature richness?

00:59:09.556 --> 00:59:11.325
&gt;&gt; Yes, in a way.

00:59:11.326 --> 00:59:13.460
&gt;&gt; It's a real tension
that I feel on

00:59:13.461 --> 00:59:15.724
my team because
owning the platform,

00:59:15.725 --> 00:59:18.700
I am ultimately responsible
for the response time of

00:59:18.701 --> 00:59:20.910
the server but
all the feature team

00:59:20.911 --> 00:59:22.540
just want to get
their features out the door.

00:59:22.541 --> 00:59:26.015
So, they're coding away
and yes, it's a problem.

00:59:26.016 --> 00:59:27.800
&gt;&gt; We can not only be reactive,

00:59:27.801 --> 00:59:29.510
we need to be proactive
to some extent in

00:59:29.511 --> 00:59:32.030
latency. That is trade-off

00:59:32.031 --> 00:59:34.520
&gt;&gt; Yes, the flights run
at a low percentage

00:59:34.521 --> 00:59:36.340
so doesn't always
hit at our 95th,

00:59:36.341 --> 00:59:38.410
the egregious ones
do and we work with

00:59:38.411 --> 00:59:39.650
the partner teams to help improve

00:59:39.651 --> 00:59:41.040
their latency in those cases.

00:59:41.041 --> 00:59:42.330
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:59:49.210 --> 00:59:52.020
&gt;&gt; Kind of a slightly
different angle

00:59:52.021 --> 00:59:53.620
on the same question.

00:59:53.621 --> 00:59:56.605
This might be a little bit naive,

00:59:56.606 --> 01:00:00.040
but as you're using metrics and

01:00:00.041 --> 01:00:03.020
you're rapidly
releasing new features,

01:00:03.021 --> 01:00:06.530
do you end up in
a local gradient descent where

01:00:06.531 --> 01:00:08.460
you're improving whatever metrics

01:00:08.461 --> 01:00:10.110
you're trying to improve?

01:00:10.111 --> 01:00:11.180
You're getting a little bit

01:00:11.181 --> 01:00:13.780
better and you find that
you're in this spot

01:00:13.781 --> 01:00:18.730
where you found a product
that you want to optimize,

01:00:18.731 --> 01:00:20.160
but you can't jump out of

01:00:20.161 --> 01:00:22.850
that minimum because
any particular new feature that

01:00:22.851 --> 01:00:24.810
you add is going to make
the experience worse

01:00:24.811 --> 01:00:27.290
before you can get over
the hump and make it better.

01:00:27.291 --> 01:00:29.390
Kind of the thing that prevents,

01:00:29.391 --> 01:00:33.775
it's what causes disruptions
to be so disruptive

01:00:33.776 --> 01:00:35.620
because there are
totally new paradigm

01:00:35.621 --> 01:00:38.015
for whatever it is
you're competing with.

01:00:38.016 --> 01:00:39.950
It's hard to get out of
that if you're doing

01:00:39.951 --> 01:00:42.715
an incremental feature
improvement along the way.

01:00:42.716 --> 01:00:44.570
That might be a naive way

01:00:44.571 --> 01:00:45.980
of thinking about
the product development,

01:00:45.981 --> 01:00:47.730
but it's something
that I wonder as

01:00:47.731 --> 01:00:50.290
I hear your presentations.

01:00:50.291 --> 01:00:51.680
As much as I think
it's awesome to

01:00:51.681 --> 01:00:53.740
get millisecond deployments
on new features,

01:00:53.741 --> 01:00:56.860
it's awesome but- thanks.

01:00:57.660 --> 01:01:01.930
&gt;&gt; I think that there's
just two different paradigms.

01:01:01.931 --> 01:01:04.135
We have to recognize that
you're not gonna get

01:01:04.136 --> 01:01:07.500
a disruption from
incremental experiments,

01:01:07.501 --> 01:01:12.505
that's why we do rely on
new products coming out

01:01:12.506 --> 01:01:15.020
that don't necessarily
have to run

01:01:15.021 --> 01:01:19.890
these experiments because
their user base is brand new.

01:01:20.700 --> 01:01:23.285
Like Teams, for example,

01:01:23.286 --> 01:01:26.315
the new product for-
that's a Slack competitor.

01:01:26.316 --> 01:01:31.270
They started out without having
any, very many customers.

01:01:31.271 --> 01:01:32.900
They do experiments now,

01:01:32.901 --> 01:01:35.605
but when they first started out,

01:01:35.606 --> 01:01:37.470
they weren't expected
to do experiments.

01:01:37.471 --> 01:01:40.800
Their expectation was
to add a new product to

01:01:40.801 --> 01:01:45.115
the ecosystem that grow
user base and then,

01:01:45.116 --> 01:01:48.055
they're expected to
optimize the experience

01:01:48.056 --> 01:01:50.355
once they have the users
to be able to do that.

01:01:50.356 --> 01:01:54.340
So, I do think there's
just different places

01:01:54.341 --> 01:01:56.530
where you apply the technologies.

01:01:56.531 --> 01:01:58.960
&gt;&gt; But it's, I think
the same technology,

01:01:58.961 --> 01:02:00.250
it's just used in
a different way,

01:02:00.251 --> 01:02:02.650
so that's where dark launches.

01:02:02.651 --> 01:02:04.350
So, it's behind a feature flag.

01:02:04.351 --> 01:02:08.345
They can develop
a brand new disruptive feature

01:02:08.346 --> 01:02:10.410
for a long period of time and be

01:02:10.411 --> 01:02:12.370
deploying and continuously
integrating it,

01:02:12.371 --> 01:02:14.270
but hiding it completely.

01:02:14.271 --> 01:02:16.370
You know, Facebook definitely
talked about that,

01:02:16.371 --> 01:02:19.400
so like Facebook Live video
or things like that.

01:02:19.401 --> 01:02:22.250
I mean, they did
this continuous deployment,

01:02:22.300 --> 01:02:26.320
and then started to experiment,

01:02:26.321 --> 01:02:28.160
and then eventually, when
they were confident enough,

01:02:28.161 --> 01:02:29.805
release it to all of us.

01:02:29.806 --> 01:02:31.890
So, it's the same technique

01:02:31.891 --> 01:02:35.360
to use for little changes
and big changes.

01:02:35.670 --> 01:02:38.650
&gt;&gt; Stands I would've
given too, dark launch.

01:02:38.651 --> 01:02:44.890
&gt;&gt; So, in Laurie's presentation,

01:02:44.891 --> 01:02:48.005
she talked briefly about
Chaos Engineering.

01:02:48.006 --> 01:02:50.440
I was wondering, does being

01:02:50.441 --> 01:02:52.560
a Microsoft Office do

01:02:52.561 --> 01:02:53.760
any sorts of these kind of

01:02:53.761 --> 01:02:55.630
resilience testing in the field?

01:02:55.631 --> 01:02:58.250
If yes, have you done anything to

01:02:58.251 --> 01:03:02.260
minimize the impact of
the failure to those users?

01:03:02.261 --> 01:03:04.885
&gt;&gt; Did you know what
Chaos Monkey is?

01:03:04.886 --> 01:03:05.320
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:03:05.321 --> 01:03:05.680
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:03:05.681 --> 01:03:06.580
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:03:06.581 --> 01:03:09.745
&gt;&gt; Yes. Short answer, yes.

01:03:09.746 --> 01:03:12.725
We have multiple
different layers.

01:03:12.726 --> 01:03:14.640
We do defense in depth, right?

01:03:14.641 --> 01:03:17.405
So, we run all the tests
that we can upfront,

01:03:17.406 --> 01:03:19.555
recognizing that everything's
going to get tested.

01:03:19.556 --> 01:03:21.020
So, there are separate systems.

01:03:21.021 --> 01:03:24.620
There's one that's continually
fuzzing, the live site.

01:03:24.621 --> 01:03:26.190
Looking for issues, there's

01:03:26.191 --> 01:03:28.720
a pen testing team
that goes and tries

01:03:28.721 --> 01:03:34.550
to penetrate or defeat one or
more entry points on Bing.

01:03:34.551 --> 01:03:36.360
There is a separate system,

01:03:36.361 --> 01:03:39.190
as well that is periodically
just taking one of

01:03:39.191 --> 01:03:43.030
our machines offline and seeing
what happens as a result.

01:03:43.031 --> 01:03:45.550
So, there's many different
things that we're doing to

01:03:45.551 --> 01:03:47.330
try to guarantee for the user

01:03:47.331 --> 01:03:50.020
that we have
a consistent experience.

01:03:50.021 --> 01:03:52.200
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:03:53.440 --> 01:03:57.370
&gt;&gt; Thanks. So, I'm curious about

01:03:57.371 --> 01:03:58.800
how you got from

01:03:58.801 --> 01:04:01.530
a 90-minute build down
to a 15-minute build?

01:04:01.531 --> 01:04:03.040
Was that, I guess,

01:04:03.041 --> 01:04:04.930
this was for
the Bing team, right?

01:04:04.931 --> 01:04:07.660
Was that primarily
by migrating to

01:04:07.661 --> 01:04:09.890
a Cloud-based Build technology or

01:04:09.891 --> 01:04:12.545
did you do some
optimization elsewhere?

01:04:12.546 --> 01:04:14.380
&gt;&gt; It was a combination of both.

01:04:14.381 --> 01:04:18.170
So, there is an internal
Cloud Build system,

01:04:18.171 --> 01:04:20.755
it's called Cloud Build.

01:04:20.756 --> 01:04:22.485
&gt;&gt; Imagine that.

01:04:22.486 --> 01:04:25.010
&gt;&gt; Because they figured it
should be descriptive, right?

01:04:25.011 --> 01:04:26.100
That's the documentation,

01:04:26.101 --> 01:04:28.100
actually it's just a file build.

01:04:29.040 --> 01:04:31.270
There's a certain
amount of caching,

01:04:31.271 --> 01:04:32.520
as well of previous targets

01:04:32.521 --> 01:04:34.030
and they will pull in the cache.

01:04:34.031 --> 01:04:35.900
A version of that has been

01:04:35.901 --> 01:04:37.470
enabled for dev machine as well,

01:04:37.471 --> 01:04:38.630
because the dev machine
doesn't have

01:04:38.631 --> 01:04:40.305
multiple build servers, right?

01:04:40.306 --> 01:04:41.510
But they will still use

01:04:41.511 --> 01:04:43.380
the remote cache and

01:04:43.381 --> 01:04:45.415
rebuild only the things
that we need.

01:04:45.416 --> 01:04:48.670
But yet, there were
major changes in the way

01:04:48.671 --> 01:04:51.895
that the code is laid
out in our system.

01:04:51.896 --> 01:04:54.395
We have been consistently
moving stuff

01:04:54.396 --> 01:04:57.220
out that the developers
don't care about.

01:04:57.221 --> 01:04:59.920
In fact, there's even a flight
for a local build

01:04:59.921 --> 01:05:03.240
that doesn't build
the test projects,

01:05:03.241 --> 01:05:04.960
so that they can
get a faster build.

01:05:04.961 --> 01:05:07.640
Yes, it's again, like with
anything else that I say,

01:05:07.641 --> 01:05:08.855
it's going to be
defense in depth.

01:05:08.856 --> 01:05:10.330
There's Cloud Build servers,

01:05:10.331 --> 01:05:12.230
there's local stuff,
we've done a bunch

01:05:12.231 --> 01:05:15.140
of optimizations
that could cleanup.

01:05:15.141 --> 01:05:17.200
It's an ongoing process.

01:05:17.201 --> 01:05:20.370
&gt;&gt; So, is there hope for
the five-hour Office build?

01:05:20.371 --> 01:05:21.155
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:05:21.156 --> 01:05:26.490
&gt;&gt; All right.

01:05:26.491 --> 01:05:29.005
&gt;&gt; But to be fair,
models that size,

01:05:29.006 --> 01:05:31.860
you're only going to get
it down so far, right?

01:05:31.861 --> 01:05:32.170
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:05:32.171 --> 01:05:33.650
&gt;&gt; This is where a remote build

01:05:33.651 --> 01:05:36.175
actually would help
them more than with us.

01:05:36.176 --> 01:05:38.490
In the initial days,
Cloud Build was great for us,

01:05:38.491 --> 01:05:39.760
but now that we've minimized it

01:05:39.761 --> 01:05:41.380
as much as we can
on the dev machine,

01:05:41.381 --> 01:05:43.350
the paralyzed build servers

01:05:43.351 --> 01:05:46.440
isn't helping us that much as.

01:05:49.620 --> 01:05:53.765
&gt;&gt; Yes. Sorry, it's okay.

01:05:53.766 --> 01:05:56.290
&gt;&gt; Yes. This discussion

01:05:56.291 --> 01:05:59.720
of all the code behind
the feature of F5,

01:05:59.721 --> 01:06:01.400
made me wonder about security.

01:06:01.401 --> 01:06:04.670
It seems like there's
a different surface area

01:06:04.671 --> 01:06:05.960
now for security texts.

01:06:05.961 --> 01:06:08.210
You can change the value
of a flag and now you

01:06:08.211 --> 01:06:10.730
expose code that wasn't
intended to be run.

01:06:10.731 --> 01:06:12.510
Has there been work in
that space or even see

01:06:12.511 --> 01:06:15.430
any real attacks in that space?

01:06:15.431 --> 01:06:18.130
&gt;&gt; All right. I
understand what you're

01:06:18.131 --> 01:06:20.000
saying and I have
not heard of that,

01:06:20.001 --> 01:06:22.425
but I think it isn't real threat.

01:06:22.426 --> 01:06:25.180
You just need to get
to the API, right?

01:06:25.181 --> 01:06:26.770
Somehow, which is how

01:06:26.771 --> 01:06:28.495
testing and production
often happen.

01:06:28.496 --> 01:06:31.470
They get into the API

01:06:31.471 --> 01:06:33.270
without going through
user interface,

01:06:33.271 --> 01:06:34.960
so the threat isn't there.

01:06:34.961 --> 01:06:38.075
I haven't heard any of
attacks that way though.

01:06:38.076 --> 01:06:40.965
&gt;&gt; You're monitoring,
everything goes smoothly,

01:06:40.966 --> 01:06:42.660
you would see a flag that
is on and you were like,

01:06:42.661 --> 01:06:44.220
this is not ever
supposed to be on.

01:06:44.221 --> 01:06:45.280
So, there's a lot,

01:06:45.281 --> 01:06:47.300
I think, related to the toggles.

01:06:47.301 --> 01:06:49.310
There's a lot of
monitoring of them.

01:06:49.311 --> 01:06:51.690
&gt;&gt; Right, yes. So when
I talk about security,

01:06:51.691 --> 01:06:54.670
I talk about
the instrumentation and

01:06:54.671 --> 01:06:58.360
experimentation as a way to
say, "This is not right."

01:06:58.361 --> 01:07:01.560
Whatever's happening right
now is not normal and then,

01:07:01.561 --> 01:07:03.985
you might shut
something off to react,

01:07:03.986 --> 01:07:07.230
but it also helps from
a security standpoint. Yes.

01:07:07.231 --> 01:07:09.880
&gt;&gt; The security
starts with making

01:07:09.881 --> 01:07:13.530
sure only certain people have
the ability to set flags.

01:07:13.960 --> 01:07:16.290
Like, none of you are

01:07:16.291 --> 01:07:17.825
going to be able to
go home and try to

01:07:17.826 --> 01:07:19.910
force a specific experiment

01:07:19.911 --> 01:07:21.805
or a feature for
being in our office.

01:07:21.806 --> 01:07:23.440
Just not the way it
works like we only

01:07:23.441 --> 01:07:25.245
allow that to happen
within our own machines.

01:07:25.246 --> 01:07:27.060
It's controlled by IP.

01:07:27.061 --> 01:07:31.530
Now, we have a way from
my office, sitting in my office,

01:07:31.531 --> 01:07:33.020
which is inside
Microsoft's Cortana,

01:07:33.021 --> 01:07:35.140
I could force myself onto
an experiment but it's

01:07:35.141 --> 01:07:38.530
only enabled for those people.

01:07:39.990 --> 01:07:43.930
&gt;&gt; Hi, thanks. So, this shift
from no experimentation to

01:07:43.931 --> 01:07:46.900
virtually continuous
experimentation that

01:07:46.901 --> 01:07:48.280
you are going through,

01:07:48.281 --> 01:07:49.865
seems to suggest to me that

01:07:49.866 --> 01:07:51.980
you also need a shift
in mindset of

01:07:51.981 --> 01:07:55.580
your engineers from
an engineering centric mindset

01:07:55.581 --> 01:07:57.080
to a scientist centric mindset,

01:07:57.081 --> 01:07:59.005
somebody that thinks
about experiments.

01:07:59.006 --> 01:08:01.550
Do you see that in
your employees,

01:08:01.551 --> 01:08:07.900
and did your recruitment change
to accommodate this more?

01:08:10.770 --> 01:08:16.170
&gt;&gt; The culture of experimentation
has definitely changed.

01:08:16.171 --> 01:08:18.020
People thinking about putting

01:08:18.021 --> 01:08:20.075
their changes behind
the flight has changed,

01:08:20.076 --> 01:08:21.460
whereas before they
would just push it,

01:08:21.461 --> 01:08:23.504
now they actually understand,

01:08:23.505 --> 01:08:25.230
"Let's do this incrementally
and make sure we

01:08:25.231 --> 01:08:28.065
don't mess up the live site
for everybody else."

01:08:28.066 --> 01:08:31.130
There is so much detail on

01:08:31.131 --> 01:08:33.785
our scorecards that
it would stagger you,

01:08:33.786 --> 01:08:36.220
even faculty, even the
academia you'd be staggered

01:08:36.221 --> 01:08:38.070
by how many metrics we have

01:08:38.071 --> 01:08:40.395
available on
a scorecard for Bing.

01:08:40.396 --> 01:08:44.600
So, they highlight the ones

01:08:44.601 --> 01:08:46.055
with the highest
confidence interval

01:08:46.056 --> 01:08:48.305
and they make decisions
based on that.

01:08:48.306 --> 01:08:51.565
&gt;&gt; I was asking how has your
recruitment process changed,

01:08:51.566 --> 01:08:54.380
to accommodate for people
that think this way,

01:08:54.381 --> 01:08:56.580
that think in terms
of experiments as

01:08:56.581 --> 01:08:59.770
opposed to people that think
in terms of code perhaps.

01:08:59.771 --> 01:09:02.070
&gt;&gt; I don't think
recruiting has changed

01:09:02.071 --> 01:09:04.060
much since it's learned
experience, right?

01:09:04.061 --> 01:09:05.660
Like we're hiring for

01:09:05.661 --> 01:09:06.820
their technical talent and they

01:09:06.821 --> 01:09:08.880
learned flighting on the way.

01:09:08.881 --> 01:09:09.920
&gt;&gt; But we had a big transition

01:09:09.921 --> 01:09:12.814
from testing data scientists,

01:09:12.815 --> 01:09:15.085
I mean in terms of HR, didn't we?

01:09:15.086 --> 01:09:17.240
I mean that, in Microsoft,

01:09:17.241 --> 01:09:18.790
my understanding was like a lot

01:09:18.791 --> 01:09:21.340
of folks who were doing testing,

01:09:21.341 --> 01:09:23.010
the testing discipline
became more

01:09:23.011 --> 01:09:24.930
oriented toward data science

01:09:24.931 --> 01:09:26.740
and actually we had
job title changes

01:09:26.741 --> 01:09:29.580
and all that sort of
thing happen, yes?

01:09:30.510 --> 01:09:33.800
I thought we went through
a pretty big transition.

01:09:33.801 --> 01:09:36.110
&gt;&gt; I can talk to that in

01:09:36.111 --> 01:09:38.635
terms of being on
an experimentation team,

01:09:38.636 --> 01:09:41.035
for Bing and for Office.

01:09:41.036 --> 01:09:47.360
The culture is hard but let's
put that hiring part aside.

01:09:47.361 --> 01:09:50.395
A culture change
is very difficult.

01:09:50.396 --> 01:09:53.440
In Bing, that's the way
they live in breath,

01:09:53.441 --> 01:09:57.320
engineers there know when
the experimentation review is,

01:09:57.321 --> 01:09:58.580
they know how to go there,

01:09:58.581 --> 01:10:00.280
they know how to
intelligently have

01:10:00.281 --> 01:10:02.275
a conversation about P values,

01:10:02.276 --> 01:10:04.870
and what statistics means.

01:10:04.871 --> 01:10:09.780
In Office, it's very
much further away.

01:10:09.781 --> 01:10:13.360
One, the set of metrics
is not as stable,

01:10:13.361 --> 01:10:15.610
it's not largely
recognized across

01:10:15.611 --> 01:10:18.620
the organization that those set
of metrics are important,

01:10:18.621 --> 01:10:22.600
and they are shape
blocking or not.

01:10:22.601 --> 01:10:25.990
So, we're working through
that with Office and just

01:10:25.991 --> 01:10:30.580
educating both the IC engineers,

01:10:30.581 --> 01:10:32.650
as well as the managers about how

01:10:32.651 --> 01:10:35.680
it works and why it's important.

01:10:35.681 --> 01:10:40.195
To Tom's point
about data science,

01:10:40.196 --> 01:10:41.770
we have sets of

01:10:41.771 --> 01:10:44.450
data scientists with
varying different backgrounds.

01:10:44.451 --> 01:10:48.165
There's testers that were
turned into data scientists.

01:10:48.166 --> 01:10:49.750
I would say that some of

01:10:49.751 --> 01:10:52.045
their statistical
knowledge is less,

01:10:52.046 --> 01:10:55.365
than say data scientists
that we hired that has

01:10:55.366 --> 01:10:57.330
no computer science
background but is

01:10:57.331 --> 01:11:00.135
a biology major, for example.

01:11:00.136 --> 01:11:02.705
So there's varying degrees of

01:11:02.706 --> 01:11:06.030
statistical ability,
in that sense.

01:11:06.031 --> 01:11:10.215
Hiring wise though,
as a developer,

01:11:10.216 --> 01:11:13.015
you need the skill sets of

01:11:13.016 --> 01:11:16.600
just being able to
code in C++ build,

01:11:16.601 --> 01:11:20.800
be diligent, be able to go
deep, understand architecture.

01:11:20.801 --> 01:11:23.000
The statistics part is important

01:11:23.001 --> 01:11:26.405
but it isn't what we would
necessarily hire for.

01:11:26.406 --> 01:11:28.380
Data savviness is probably

01:11:28.381 --> 01:11:31.635
something that we would
look for or ask about.

01:11:31.636 --> 01:11:34.020
&gt;&gt; I mean not just developers,

01:11:34.021 --> 01:11:37.550
but also someone who they
call a product owner.

01:11:37.551 --> 01:11:39.650
I mean, there is
a definite shift there,

01:11:39.651 --> 01:11:41.865
and this is among some of
the companies who are now,

01:11:41.866 --> 01:11:43.080
there is a hypothesis.

01:11:43.081 --> 01:11:44.470
So, you want this function,

01:11:44.471 --> 01:11:47.240
you want this new feature,
what's your hypothesis?

01:11:47.241 --> 01:11:49.240
What would change
if you got that?

01:11:49.241 --> 01:11:51.090
Let's specify that as a metric.

01:11:51.091 --> 01:11:53.945
So, that's like a whole
different type of process.

01:11:53.946 --> 01:11:55.450
Sometimes product owners are

01:11:55.451 --> 01:11:58.370
technical people but sometimes
they are business-oriented

01:11:58.371 --> 01:11:59.530
but they have to have

01:11:59.531 --> 01:12:02.820
this new mindset to deal
with experimentation.

01:12:02.821 --> 01:12:05.655
&gt;&gt; You could even turn
that question around and say,

01:12:05.656 --> 01:12:08.770
"Do you need experts
in data science?

01:12:08.840 --> 01:12:11.460
Could your
experimentation platform

01:12:11.461 --> 01:12:12.550
be made simple enough that

01:12:12.551 --> 01:12:16.235
anyone could understand it
without data science degree?"

01:12:16.236 --> 01:12:18.650
That's what we aim for and anyone

01:12:18.651 --> 01:12:20.735
should be able to do
an experiment in Bing.

01:12:20.736 --> 01:12:22.350
That's not always the case but.

01:12:22.351 --> 01:12:24.425
&gt;&gt; I'm just looking at
the time, so last question.

01:12:24.426 --> 01:12:24.970
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:12:24.971 --> 01:12:28.855
&gt;&gt; More importantly
the data science

01:12:28.856 --> 01:12:32.355
reminds me very much statistics.

01:12:32.356 --> 01:12:35.440
There's no surprise that in

01:12:35.441 --> 01:12:39.750
schools you have bio-statistics,

01:12:39.751 --> 01:12:43.395
you have physical
statistics for physics,

01:12:43.396 --> 01:12:46.880
you have engineering statistics
of different discipline

01:12:46.881 --> 01:12:54.165
because data depends on
how you collect the data,

01:12:54.166 --> 01:12:58.645
what representation
you make in the data,

01:12:58.646 --> 01:13:00.870
whether it's
symbolic representation,

01:13:00.871 --> 01:13:01.920
whether it's numeric,

01:13:01.921 --> 01:13:06.135
whether it's any
other representation.

01:13:06.136 --> 01:13:09.705
Then, what questions
you are going to ask.

01:13:09.706 --> 01:13:12.135
That depends on the domain.

01:13:12.136 --> 01:13:15.170
So, yes, computer scientists have

01:13:15.171 --> 01:13:18.355
a role there but they just have,

01:13:18.356 --> 01:13:23.315
I don't want to put any number
but they just have a role.

01:13:23.316 --> 01:13:29.810
But it really is a
discipline dependent,

01:13:29.811 --> 01:13:33.155
what data is going

01:13:33.156 --> 01:13:38.065
to be collected and what
questions will you be asking.

01:13:38.066 --> 01:13:40.555
&gt;&gt; Or product dependent, right?

01:13:40.556 --> 01:13:42.275
The products are different.

01:13:42.276 --> 01:13:45.125
MSN set of data scientists

01:13:45.126 --> 01:13:49.340
have a different product
than Office does.

01:13:49.341 --> 01:13:52.500
So, you have to be able to
ask the right questions.

01:13:52.501 --> 01:13:54.170
What does it mean in Office,

01:13:54.171 --> 01:13:57.580
in terms of subscription users
and enterprise users

01:13:57.581 --> 01:14:02.575
versus MSN is
a primarily media based?

01:14:02.576 --> 01:14:06.070
That must be a little challenge
for a company like

01:14:06.071 --> 01:14:10.815
Microsoft that wants
to sell a platform,

01:14:10.816 --> 01:14:15.420
that is covers
a much broader audience.

01:14:15.421 --> 01:14:17.945
Nevertheless, you'll have to

01:14:17.946 --> 01:14:22.190
be realistic, what to expect.

01:14:22.191 --> 01:14:25.330
&gt;&gt; I think on that point
can we thank speakers

01:14:25.331 --> 01:14:30.960
again? Thank you
very much for coming

